 which non‑negative integers \\(k\\) admit a set \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal{L}\\) are sunny.\n\nBelow we construct examples for the two extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Construction with **no** sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the three families of lines that are *not* sunny:\n\n* **Horizontal lines**: \\(y=c\\) for integer \\(c\\);\n* **Vertical lines**: \\(x=c\\) for integer \\(c\\);\n* **Anti‑diagonal lines**: \\(x+y=c\\) for integer \\(c\\).\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) have integer coordinates, hence each belongs to exactly one line of each family.  \nWe now select \\(n\\) lines among these three families so that every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) is covered.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_0=\n\\bigl\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y_{h}\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x_{v}\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere  \n\n\\[\nh=\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil,\\qquad\nv=n-h.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|\\mathcal{L}_0|=h+v+1=n\\).  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, so none is sunny.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\).  \nIf \\(b\\le h\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).  \nOtherwise \\(b>h\\). Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v,\n\\]\n\nso the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is in \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\) because \\(a\\le v\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).  \nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_0\\).\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) lines with **exactly** \\(k=0\\) sunny lines satisfying the required covering property.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Construction with **all** lines sunny (\\(k=n\