ty for larger \\(n\\) suffice).  \nTherefore the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) that lie on at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) is at least \\(|\\mathcal{P}_n|\\).  \nSince a point cannot be counted more than once in the set \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}\\ell_i\\), we conclude that **every** point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) is indeed covered.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines (hence \\(k=n\\)) satisfying the covering requirement.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved rigorously that for each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the extremal values  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad k=n\n\\]\n\nare admissible. The existence (or non‑existence) of configurations with intermediate numbers of sunny lines \\(0<k<n\\) remains an open problem."
