distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Extremal constructions  \n\n#### 2.1  Construction with \\(k=0\\) (no sunny line)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad v=n-h .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{\\,y=1,\\dots ,y=h\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence none is sunny, and \\(|\\mathcal L_{0}|=h+v+1=n\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le h\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(b>h\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v\\); consequently the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_{0}\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n#### 2.2  Construction with \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + 1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny. The slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct. Set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n}= \\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{i}{i+1}a + 1\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n(i+1)b = i a + (i+1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\gcd(i,i+1)=1\\), (5) holds for integers \\(a,b\\) exactly when  \n\n\\[\na\\equiv -1 \\pmod{i+1},\\qquad\nb = i\\Bigl(\\frac{a+1}{i+1}\\Bigr)+1 .\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(a=(i+1)q-1\\) with \\(q\\ge1\\); then \\(b=iq+1\\).  \nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(i+1)q-1 + iq+1 \\le n+1\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n(2i+1)q \\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence for a fixed \\(i\\) the number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on \\(\\ell_i\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nN_i=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSumming (6) over \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} N_i\n\\; \\ge\\;\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{n+1}{2i+1} - n\n\\;=\\;\n(n+1)\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\frac1{2i+1} - n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe series \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\frac1{2i+1}\\) exceeds the integral  \n\n\\[\n\\int_{1}^{n+1}\\frac{dx}{2x+1}\n   =\\frac12\\ln\\frac{2n+3}{3}.\n\\]\n\nThus the right\u2013hand side of (7) is larger than  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1}{2}\\ln\\frac{2n+3}{3}-n .\n\\]\n\nA direct check for \\(n=3,4,5\\) shows this quantity already exceeds \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) (the size of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) from (1)).  \nFor larger \\(n\\) the function \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\ln\\frac{2n+3}{3}-n\\) grows faster than \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\), so the inequality holds for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{n} N_i \\ge |\\mathcal P_n|.\n\\]\n\nSince each point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) can belong to at most one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\) (different slopes give distinct intersection points), the inequality forces equality; therefore **every** point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n\\).\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Reduction Lemma  \n\nWe now show that from any admissible configuration we can decrease the number of sunny lines by one while preserving all requirements.\n\n> **Lemma 3.1**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines.  \n> Then there exists another set \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nPick a sunny line \\(s\\in\\mathcal L\\). Because \\(s\\) is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, its slope belongs to one of the three open intervals  \n\n\\[\n(-\\infty,-1),\\qquad (-1,0),\\qquad (0,\\infty).\n\\]\n\nConsider the three families of non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in\\mathbb Z\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each point \\(P\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on \\(s\\) choose **one** of the three non\u2011sunny lines that also passes through \\(P\\) (this is always possible because the three families together contain a line through any lattice point). Let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N =\\{\\,\\text{the chosen non\u2011sunny line for each }P\\in s\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\mathcal N\\) is a finite multiset of non\u2011sunny lines; discard duplicates, obtaining a set \\(\\mathcal N'\\) of at most \\(|s\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines. Replace \\(s\\) in \\(\\mathcal L\\) by the lines of \\(\\mathcal N'\\).  \n\nBecause every point of \\(s\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\) is now covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal N'\\), and all other points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) were already covered by the remaining lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\), the new family still covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n\nThe total number of lines after replacement is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L'| = |\\mathcal L|-1+|\\mathcal N'|\n               \\le n-1+|s\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\n               \\le n,\n\\]\n\nthe last inequality because a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies in the triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\)). If \\(|\\mathcal L'|<n\\) we may add arbitrary distinct non\u2011sunny lines (for instance unused horizontal lines) until the total becomes exactly \\(n\\).  \n\nAll added lines are non\u2011sunny, so the number of sunny lines has decreased by exactly one. \u220e\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Induction on the number of sunny lines  \n\nWe have exhibited configurations for \\(k=0\\) (Section\u202f2.1) and for \\(k=n\\) (Section\u202f2.2).  \n\nAssume that for some \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \nApplying Lemma\u202f3.1 to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) yields a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) with exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.  \n\nThus, starting from the known case \\(k=n\\) and iterating the reduction lemma, we obtain configurations for every integer \\(k\\) down to \\(0\\).  \n\nConversely, starting from the case \\(k=0\\) and adding sunny lines (for instance the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) of Section\u202f2.2 one by one, while discarding an equal number of non\u2011sunny lines) we can also build configurations for any larger \\(k\\). Hence **all** integers between the extremes are attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Final conclusion  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines in the plane such that  \n\n* each point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nTherefore the complete answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor any given \\(n\\ge3\\). \u220e"
