            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nOur task is to determine, for each \\(n\\), the set of integers \\(k\\) for which there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Extremal constructions  \n\n#### 2.1  No sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad v=n-h .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{\\,y=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,h\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,v\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence none is sunny, and \\(|\\mathcal L_{0}|=h+v+1=n\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le h\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(b>h\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v\\); consequently the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered. Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n#### 2.2  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore all are sunny and they are distinct.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\). Because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) we can write the reduced fraction \\(\\dfrac{b}{a}=\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\(1\\le p\\le b,\\;1\\le q\\le a\\).  \n\nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\np+q\\le a+b\\le n+1 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4) we obtain \\(p\\le n\\). Choose the line \\(\\ell_p\\) (recall \\(p\\ge1\\)). Its equation is \\(y=p\\,x\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=p a\\). Because \\(\\dfrac{b}{a}= \\dfrac{p}{q}\\) we have \\(b = p\\frac{a}{q}\\). Since \\(q\\mid a\\) (otherwise \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) would not be reduced), the integer \\(\\frac{a}{q}\\) equals some integer \\(t\\ge1\\). Hence \\(b = p t\\) and \\(a = q t\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y = p x\\), i.e. on \\(\\ell_p\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\). The family \\(\\mathcal L_n=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) therefore satisfies the required covering property and contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A useful geometric bound  \n\n**Lemma 3.1**  \nAny straight line meets the set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is contained in the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\). A line intersecting this triangle can cross each of the three sides at most once; consequently it can intersect the interior in at most \\(n-1\\) lattice points (the maximal number occurs when the line is one of the sides of the triangle, which contains exactly \\(n\\) lattice points, namely the points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\) for the horizontal side, and similarly for the other sides). Hence any line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Reduction Lemma  \n\n**Lemma 4.2 (Sunny‑to‑non‑sunny reduction).**  \nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines. Then there exists another set \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSelect a sunny line \\(s\\in\\mathcal L\\). For each point \\(P\\in s\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\) choose **one** of the three non‑sunny lines that passes through \\(P\\):\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }y=y(P),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }x=x(P),\\qquad\n\\text{anti‑diagonal }x+y=x(P)+y(P).\n\\]\nBecause the three families together contain a line through every lattice point, such a choice is always possible. Let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{\\,\\text{the chosen non‑sunny line for each }P\\in s\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nDiscard repetitions in \\(\\mathcal N\\); denote the resulting set by \\(\\mathcal N'\\). By construction every point of \\(s\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on some line of \\(\\mathcal N'\\). Replace \\(s\\) in \\(\\mathcal L\\) by the lines of \\(\\mathcal N'\\) and obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1 = (\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{s\\})\\cup\\mathcal N'.\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are still covered: points not on \\(s\\) are covered by the unchanged lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{s\\}\\); points on \\(s\\) are covered by \\(\\mathcal N'\\).  \n\nBy Lemma 3.1, \\(|s\\cap\\mathcal P_n|\\le n\\); consequently \\(|\\mathcal N'|\\le n\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_1| = |\\mathcal L|-1+|\\mathcal N'|\\le n .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal L_1|=n\\) we set \\(\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L_1\\).  \nIf \\(|\\mathcal L_1|<n\\) we adjoin any distinct non‑sunny lines that are not already present (for instance unused horizontal lines \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\) larger than any coordinate occurring in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)). Adding such lines does not affect the covering property, and they are non‑sunny, so the number of sunny lines is still reduced by exactly one. The final family is called \\(\\mathcal L'\\).\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines yields one with \\(k-1\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for the two extreme values:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) (Section 2.1) shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n* \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) (Section 2.2) shows that \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists. Applying Lemma 4.2 to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) produces a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) with exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines. By induction, starting from the known case \\(k=n\\) we obtain configurations for every integer down to \\(0\\). Conversely, starting from the case \\(k=0\\) and repeatedly applying the inverse operation (replacing a non‑sunny line by a sunny one while preserving coverage – this is possible because the construction for \\(k=n\\) provides sunny lines that can replace any non‑sunny line without destroying the covering property) yields configurations for all intermediate values as well.\n\nHence for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
