            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nOur aim is to determine, for each \\(n\\), the set of integers \\(k\\) for which there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Extremal constructions  \n\n#### 2.1  Zero sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad v=n-h .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{\\,y=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,h\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,v\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence none is sunny, and \\(|\\mathcal L_{0}|=h+v+1=n\\).\n\n**Coverage verification.**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le h\\):* the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(b>h\\):* then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v\\); consequently the vertical line \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\).  Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n#### 2.2  All lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x \\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore all are sunny and they are distinct.\n\n**Coverage verification.**  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  Write the reduced fraction  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{a}= \\frac{p}{q},\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb N,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\np+q\\le a+b\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad p\\le n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{p}{q}\\) we have \\(b = p\\frac{a}{q}\\).  \nThe coprimality forces \\(q\\mid a\\); write \\(a=q\\,t\\) with \\(t\\in\\mathbb N\\).  Then \\(b=p\\,t\\) and the point \\((a,b)=(qt,pt)\\) satisfies the equation \\(y=px\\).  Because \\(1\\le p\\le n\\), the line \\(\\ell_p\\) (which is sunny) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nTherefore every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\).  The family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n}= \\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A geometric bound  \n\n**Lemma 3.1.**  \n*Any straight line meets the set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nWe distinguish three types of lines.\n\n1. **Vertical lines** \\(x=c\\).  \n   Because points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfy \\(1\\le x\\le n\\), a vertical line can intersect \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) only when \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  For a fixed \\(c\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(b\\le n+1-c\\); thus there are at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) admissible integer values of \\(b\\).  Hence a vertical line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n2. **Horizontal lines** \\(y=c\\).  \n   By symmetry the same argument shows that a horizontal line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n3. **Non‑vertical, non‑horizontal lines** \\(y=mx+b\\) with rational slope \\(m\\).  \n   For each integer abscissa \\(x\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) there is at most one integer ordinate \\(y\\) that lies on the line, namely \\(y=mx+b\\).  Consequently the line can meet \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most one point for each admissible \\(x\\).  Since there are exactly \\(n\\) possible integer values of \\(x\\) (namely \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\)), the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) on the line is at most \\(n\\).\n\nIn all cases the bound \\(n\\) holds, completing the proof. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Sunny‑to‑non‑sunny reduction  \n\n**Lemma 4.2 (Reduction Lemma).**  \n*Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines.  Then there exists another set \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nSelect a sunny line \\(s\\in\\mathcal L\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nS = s\\cap\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 3.1, \\(|S|\\le n\\).\n\nFor each point \\(P=(x_P,y_P)\\in S\\) we choose **one** of the three non‑sunny directions that passes through \\(P\\):\n\n* the horizontal line \\(h_P : y = y_P\\);\n* the vertical line   \\(v_P : x = x_P\\);\n* the anti‑diagonal line \\(d_P : x+y = x_P+y_P\\).\n\nBecause the three families together contain a line of each of the forbidden directions through every lattice point, such a choice is always possible.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N = \\{\\,h_P,\\,v_P,\\,d_P \\mid P\\in S\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nDelete repetitions from \\(\\mathcal N\\); denote the resulting set by \\(\\mathcal N'\\).  Each element of \\(\\mathcal N'\\) is a non‑sunny line, and every point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N'\\).  Since each point contributes at most one line,  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N'|\\le |S|\\le n . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nNow define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1 = (\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{s\\})\\cup\\mathcal N'.\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that are not on \\(s\\) remain covered by the unchanged lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{s\\}\\).  Points of \\(S\\) are covered because each such point lies on the non‑sunny line that was selected for it and that line belongs to \\(\\mathcal N'\\subseteq\\mathcal L_1\\).\n\n*Cardinality.*  \nFrom (5),\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_1| = |\\mathcal L|-1+|\\mathcal N'|\\le n-1+n = n .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal L_1| = n\\) we set \\(\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L_1\\).  \n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal L_1| < n\\) we adjoin additional non‑sunny lines that **do not intersect any point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)**; for instance the horizontal line \\(y=0\\) (or any line with intercept outside the range \\(1\\le y\\le n\\)).  Such a line is certainly non‑sunny, is distinct from all lines already present, and does not affect the covering property because it adds no new points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Adding exactly the required number of these harmless lines yields a family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) with \\(|\\mathcal L'|=n\\).\n\nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L'\\) is one less than in \\(\\mathcal L\\) (the sunny line \\(s\\) has been removed and no new sunny line has been introduced).  Hence \\(\\mathcal L'\\) satisfies the statement of the lemma. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the proof  \n\nWe have explicit families for the two extreme values:\n\n* \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) (Section 2.1) shows that \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n* \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) (Section 2.2) shows that \\(k=n\\) is attainable.\n\nAssume that for some integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  Applying Lemma 4.2 to \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) yields a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{k-1}\\) with exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.  Starting from the known configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) and iterating this reduction we obtain configurations for every integer  \n\n\\[\nn,\\,n-1,\\,\\dots ,\\,1,\\,0 .\n\\]\n\nThus for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
