e distinct, the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot (n-1)=n^{2}-n .\n\\]\n\nIndeed, after placing the first line we may cover at most \\(n\\) points; the second line, having a different slope, can meet the first line in at most one point, so it can contribute at most \\(n-1\\) new points, and similarly each subsequent line contributes at most one fewer new point than its predecessor.  Summing the geometric series gives the bound \\(n+(n-1)+\\dots +1 = \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  However, a sharper bound is obtained by noting that any two distinct sunny lines intersect inside the convex hull of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); each intersection point belongs to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and can be counted only once.  There are \\(\\binom{n}{2}\\) intersection points, so the total number of distinct points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn + \\bigl(\\binom{n}{2}\\bigr) = n + \\frac{n(n-1)}2 = \\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nEquality would require that **every** intersection of two sunny lines be a point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and that each line contain exactly the \\(n\\) points consisting of its intersection with the other \\(n-1\\) lines together with one additional point.  This is impossible because the three forbidden directions prevent three lines from meeting pairwise inside \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) while remaining distinct and sunny.  A concrete contradiction can be exhibited for \\(n=3\\): the three sunny lines would have to intersect pairwise at the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\), but any two of those points determine a line of slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), which is not sunny.  Hence the assumed configuration cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Sunny‑to‑non‑sunny reduction  \n\n**Lemma 5.1 (Reduction Lemma).**  \n*Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines.  Then there exists another set \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose a sunny line \\(s\\in\\mathcal L\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nS = s\\cap\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 3.1, \\(|S|\\le n\\).\n\nFor each point \\(P=(x_P,y_P)\\in S\\) select **one** of the three non‑sunny directions that passes through \\(P\\):\n\n* the horizontal line \\(h_P:\\;y=y_P\\);\n* the vertical line   \\(v_P:\\;x=x_P\\);\n* the anti‑diagonal line \\(d_P:\\;x+y=x_P+y_P\\).\n\nCollect the chosen lines and delete repetitions; denote the resulting set by \\(\\mathcal N\\).  Every line in \\(\\mathcal N\\) is non‑sunny, and each point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N\\).  Because we chose at most one line per point,  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|\\le |S|\\le n . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1 = (\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{s\\})\\cup\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that are not on \\(s\\) remain covered, and the points of \\(S\\) are now covered by the lines of \\(\\mathcal N\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_1\\) still covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal L_1|=n\\) we set \\(\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L_1\\).  \nIf \\(|\\mathcal L_1|<n\\) we adjoin additional non‑sunny lines that do **not** intersect \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (for instance the horizontal line \\(y=0\\) or the vertical line \\(x=0\\)).  Adding exactly the needed number of such harmless lines yields a family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) with \\(|\\mathcal L'|=n\\).\n\nThe construction never introduces a new sunny line, and we have removed the sunny line \\(s\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal L'\\) contains exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Completion of the proof  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for \\(k=0\\) (Section 2.1) and for \\(k=n-1\\) (Section 2.2).  \nLemma 5.1 shows that from any configuration with \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines we can obtain a configuration with \\(k-1\\) sunny lines while preserving the total of \\(n\\) lines.  Starting