            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to any of  \n\n\\[\n(1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1),\n\\]\n\ni.e. its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nOur task is to determine, for each \\(n\\), the set of integers \\(k\\) for which there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Extremal constructions  \n\n#### 2.1  Zero sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad v=n-h .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{\\,y=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,h\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,v\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence none is sunny, and \\(|\\mathcal L_{0}|=h+v+1=n\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le h\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (present in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(b>h\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v\\); consequently the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered, proving the existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_i\\) have slope \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence they are sunny and distinct.  \nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(t, i t)\\mid t=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{i+1}\\rfloor\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich are at most \\(n\\) points; in fact \\(|\\ell_i\\cap\\mathcal P_n|= \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{i+1}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le n\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-1}= \\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe last line is non‑sunny (slope \\(-1\\)).  We now verify that \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) covers all of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nTake \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).  If \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point lies on the anti‑diagonal line.  Otherwise \\(a+b\\le n\\).  Set \\(i=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{b}{a}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) (note that \\(0\\le i\\le n-1\\)).  Then  \n\n\\[\ni a \\le b < (i+1)a .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a,b\\) are integers, the inequality forces \\(b=i a\\); otherwise \\(b\\) would lie strictly between two consecutive multiples of \\(a\\), impossible.  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=i a\\) and belongs to \\(\\ell_i\\).  Therefore every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\), which contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A geometric bound  \n\n**Lemma 3.1.** *Any straight line meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is vertical, say \\(x=c\\)