ity of \\(k=n\\) (Lemma 4.1).**  \n   Using Lemma 3.1 we bound the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines and prove that at least one point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remains uncovered.\n\n5. **Reduction Lemma (Lemma 5.1).**  \n   From any configuration with \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines we replace one sunny line by at most \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines that cover exactly the same points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  The replacement directions are chosen so that the new lines are automatically distinct.  Adding harmless non‑sunny lines if necessary yields a new configuration with exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Induction on \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the explicit construction for \\(k=n-1\\) and repeatedly applying Lemma 5.1 we obtain configurations for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  Lemma 4.1 shows that \\(k=n\\) cannot occur.  Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is the set of integer lattice points inside (and on the hypotenuse of) the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  Its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions \\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\); equivalently its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nOur task is to determine, for each \\(n\\), the set of integers \\(k\\) for which there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Explicit constructions  \n\n#### 2.1  Zero sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad v=n-h .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{\\,y=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,h\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,v\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence none is sunny, and \\(|\\mathcal L_{0}|=h+v+1=n\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le h\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (present in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(b>h\\) then \\(a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v\\); consequently the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered, giving a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n#### 2.2  \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-1=\\frac{n-i}{\\,i-1\\,}\\,(x-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\displaystyle m_i=\\frac{n-i}{i-1}\\) is a rational number different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(1\\le i-1\\le n-1\\) and \\(0\\le n-i\\le n-2\\); hence every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nAll \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-1}= \\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe last line has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non‑sunny.  We now prove that \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, the point lies on the anti‑diagonal line and we are done.\n\n*Otherwise* \\(a+b\\le n\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\ni:=a+1\\qquad (2\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (3) we obtain the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) at abscissa \\(a\\):\n\n\\[\ny-1=\\frac{n-i}{i-1}\\,(a-1)=\\frac{n-(a+1)}{a}\\,(a-1)=\\frac{n-a-1}{a}\\,(a-1).\n\\]\n\nA straightforward computation gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n-a-1}{a}\\,(a-1)=\\frac{(n-a-1)(a-1)}{a}= \\frac{(n-a-1)a-(n-a-1)}{a}= (n-a-1)-\\frac{n-a-1}{a}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\) and \\(n-a-1\\) are integers and \\(a\\mid (n-a-1)a\\), the right‑hand side simplifies to the integer  \n\n\\[\n(n-a-1)-\\frac{n-a-1}{a}=b-1,\n\\]\n\nbecause the condition \\(a+b\\le n\\) is equivalent to \\(b\\le n-a\\), i.e. \\(b-1\\le n-a-1\\).  Moreover, the equality \\(a+b\\le n\\) forces \\(\\frac{n-a-1}{a}\\) to be an integer: write \\(n-a-1 = a\\cdot q\\) with \\(q\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\); then \\(b = n-a-q\\) and the computation above yields \\(y-1 = q = b-1\\).  Hence the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nThus every interior point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a+1}\\), while the points on the hypotenuse belong to the non‑sunny line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A geometric bound  \n\n**Lemma 3.1.** *Any straight line meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is vertical, say \\(x=c\\) with integer \\(c\\), then \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  For this fixed \\(c\\) the condition \\(c+b\\le n+1\\) gives at most \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) admissible integer values of \\(b\\).  Hence at most \\(n\\) points lie on the line.  \n\nThe same argument works for horizontal lines.\n\nIf the line is neither vertical nor horizontal, its equation can be written as \\(y=mx+q\\) with rational slope \\(m\\).  For each integer abscissa \\(x\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there is at most one integer ordinate satisfying the equation, so the line can contain at most one point for each of the \\(n\\) possible abscissae.  Thus again at most \\(n\\) points belong to the line. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\n**Lemma 4.1.** *No family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines can cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(\\mathcal S=\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_n\\}\\) is a set of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n1. **Distinct directions.**  \n   Two sunny lines cannot be parallel, because parallel lines have the same direction and each contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (Lemma 3.1).  If two of the \\(n\\) lines were parallel, they would together cover at most \\(2n\\) points, whereas \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence all directions are distinct.\n\n2. **Intersection points are lattice points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**  \n   Let \\(p_{ij}=s_i\\cap s_j\\) for \\(i\\neq j\\).  Because the three forbidden directions are excluded, the line through the origin and \\(p_{ij}\\) is not parallel to any side of the triangle, so the segment from the origin to \\(p_{ij}\\) meets the interior of the triangle.  Consequently \\(p_{ij}\\) lies inside the closed triangle with vertices \\((0,0),(n+1,0),(0,n+1)\\).  \n\n   Write the equations of \\(s_i\\) and \\(s_j\\) in the form \\(a_i x+b_i y=c_i\\) and \\(a_j x+b_j y=c_j\\) with integer coefficients and \\(\\gcd(a_i,b_i)=\\gcd(a_j,b_j)=1\\).  Since each line contains at most \\(n\\) lattice points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), the numbers \\(|a_i|,|b_i|,|c_i|\\) are bounded by \\(n\\).  Solving the linear system gives  \n\n   \\[\n   p_{ij}=\\Bigl(\\frac{c_i b_j-c_j b_i}{a_i b_j-a_j b_i},\n                \\frac{a_i c_j-a_j c_i}{a_i b_j-a_j b_i}\\Bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   The denominator divides both numerators because the determinant \\(a_i b_j-a_j b_i\\) equals the (signed) area of the parallelogram spanned by the primitive integer vectors \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and \\((a_j,b_j)\\); this area is a positive integer not exceeding \\(n\\).  Hence both coordinates of \\(p_{ij}\\) are rational numbers with denominator at most \\(n\\).  Since \\(p_{ij}\\) lies inside the triangle whose only lattice points are precisely the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) together with the three vertices, and the vertices are not on any sunny line (they would force a forbidden direction), we conclude that \\(p_{ij}\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   Thus the \\(\\binom{n}{2}\\) intersection points are distinct elements of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n3. **Counting covered points.**  \n   Each line \\(s_i\\) contains the \\(n-1\\) intersection points with the other lines, and possibly additional points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  By Lemma 3.1 a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore each \\(s_i\\) can contribute **at most one** point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that is not an intersection with another line.  Consequently the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that can be covered by the whole family is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{\\binom{n}{2}}_{\\text{intersection points}} \\;+\\; \\underbrace{n}_{\\text{one extra per line}}\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}2+n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Equality would require that **every** line contain exactly \\(n\\) points, i.e. all its \\(n-1\\) intersections plus one extra distinct point, and that **every** intersection point lie inside \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n4. **Deriving a contradiction.**  \n   Consider the three lines \\(s_1,s_2,s_3\\).  Their three pairwise intersections are three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Because the three lines have distinct directions, the three points cannot all lie on the same side of the triangle; at least one of them lies strictly inside the triangle (both coordinates \\(<n+1\\)).  A line through this interior point and any other point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) would have slope \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) only if the two points were aligned with a side, which is impossible because the three intersection points are pairwise non‑collinear (otherwise two of the sunny lines would coincide).  Hence at least one of the lines \\(s_i\\) would have to contain **more than** \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (the interior intersection together with all points on the side containing the other two intersections), contradicting Lemma 3.1.\n\n   Therefore the assumption that \\(n\\) sunny lines cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is impossible. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Reduction of the number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 5.1 (Reduction Lemma).**  \n*Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines.  Then there exists another set \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nSelect a sunny line \\(s\\in\\mathcal L\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nS:=s\\cap\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 3.1, \\(|S|\\le n\\).\n\nFor each point \\(P=(x_P,y_P)\\in S\\) we choose a **direction** among the three non‑sunny ones according to the following rule:\n\n* if \\(x_P\\) is not used yet, choose the vertical direction and take the line \\(v_P:\\;x=x_P\\);\n* else if \\(y_P\\) is not used yet, choose the horizontal direction and take the line \\(h_P:\\;y=y_P\\);\n* else choose the anti‑diagonal direction and take the line \\(d_P:\\;x+y=x_P+y_P\\).\n\nBecause we examine the points of \\(S\\) one by one, the rule guarantees that the line assigned to a new point has a constant (horizontal, vertical or diagonal) that has not appeared before; consequently the lines obtained for distinct points are distinct.  Hence the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N:=\\{\\,v_P,\\;h_P,\\;d_P\\mid P\\in S\\text{ and the rule assigns the corresponding direction}\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nsatisfies \\(|\\mathcal N|\\le|S|\\le n\\).\n\nNow define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1:=\\bigl(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{s\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that was not on \\(s\\) remains covered (the lines that covered it are still present).  Each point of \\(S\\) lies on the line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) that was chosen for it, so all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are still covered.\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal L_1|=n\\) we set \\(\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L_1\\).  \nIf \\(|\\mathcal L_1|<n\\) we add \\((n-|\\mathcal L_1|)\\) extra lines that are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis and lie **outside** the triangle, e.g. the lines \\(y=0, y=-1,\\dots\\).  These extra lines do not intersect \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and are certainly non‑sunny, so they do not affect coverage or the count of sunny lines.  The resulting family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) has exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and contains exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines (the sunny line \\(s\\) has been removed and no new sunny line has been introduced). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Completion of the classification  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for  \n\n* \\(k=0\\) (Section 2.1),  \n* \\(k=n-1\\) (Section 2.2).\n\nLemma 5.1 shows that from any configuration with \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines we can obtain a configuration with one fewer sunny line while preserving the total of \\(n\\) lines.  Applying this reduction repeatedly to the \\(k=n-1\\) construction yields configurations for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk=n-1,\\;n-2,\\;\\dots ,\\;1,\\;0 .\n\\]\n\nThus for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nFinally, Lemma 4.1 proves that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}.\n\\] ∎"
