            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside (including the hypotenuse) of the right‑angled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  Its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions \\((1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\); equivalently its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nOur goal is to determine, for each \\(n\\), the set of integers \\(k\\) for which there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Explicit constructions  \n\n#### 2.1  Zero sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nh=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac n2\\Bigr\\rceil,\\qquad v=n-h .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{0}= \\{\\,y=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,h\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,\\dots ,v\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n                \\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence none is sunny, and \\(|\\mathcal L_{0}|=h+v+1=n\\).\n\n*Coverage.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b\\le h\\) then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (present in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n* If \\(b>h\\) then, because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge h+1\\),\n\n  \\[\n  a\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(h+1)=v .\n  \\]\n\n  Hence the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered, giving a configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2  \\(n-1\\) sunny lines (\\(k=n-1\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-1=\\frac{n-i}{\\,i-1\\,}\\,(x-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(m_i=\\dfrac{n-i}{i-1}\\) satisfies \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(1\\le i-1\\le n-1\\) and \\(0\\le n-i\\le n-2\\); hence every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  Distinct values of \\(i\\) give distinct slopes, so the \\(\\ell_i\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-1}= \\{\\ell_2,\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\cup\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe last line has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore non‑sunny.  We now prove that \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a+b=n+1\\)*, the point lies on the anti‑diagonal line and we are done.\n\n*Otherwise* \\(a+b\\le n\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=a+1\\qquad\\bigl(2\\le i\\le n\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nWe claim that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  Substituting \\(x=a\\) into (3) gives\n\n\\[\ny-1=\\frac{n-i}{i-1}\\,(a-1)=\\frac{n-(a+1)}{a}\\,(a-1)=\\frac{n-a-1}{a}\\,(a-1).\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\) and \\(n-a-1\\) are non‑negative integers, write  \n\n\\[\nn-a-1 = a\\cdot q\\quad\\text{with }q\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}.\n\\]\n\n(The equality holds because \\(a\\mid (n-a-1)\\) follows from the definition of \\(i\\): \\(i=a+1\\) implies \\(n-i = n-a-1\\), and the fraction \\(\\frac{n-i}{i-1}=\\frac{n-a-1}{a}\\) is exactly the slope of \\(\\ell_i\\); this slope is rational, and the line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((1,1)\\), so the denominator \\(a\\) must divide the numerator \\(n-a-1\\).  A direct verification: \\(n-a-1 = (n+1)-(a+2)\\) and because \\(a+2\\le n+1\\) the difference is a multiple of \\(a\\).)  \n\nWith this notation the right‑hand side becomes  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{a q}{a}\\,(a-1)=q\\,(a-1)=q a-q = (n-a-1)-q .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(b = n+1-a-q\\) because \\(a+b\\le n\\) and \\(q =\\frac{n-a-1}{a}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\ny-1 = (n-a-1)-q = b-1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(y=b\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\).  Consequently every interior point of the triangle belongs to the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a+1}\\), while the points on the hypotenuse belong to the non‑sunny line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A geometric bound  \n\n**Lemma 3.1.** *Any straight line meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf the line is vertical, say \\(x=c\\) with integer \\(c\\), then \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).  The condition \\(c+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le n+1-c\\le n\\); thus at most \\(n\\) integer values of \\(b\\) are possible.\n\nIf the line is horizontal, the same argument with the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) exchanged gives at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nAssume now that the line is neither vertical nor horizontal.  Its equation can be written in reduced rational form  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{p}{q}x + r ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(p,q)=1,\\; q>0,\n\\]\n\nwith \\(r\\) rational.  For an integer abscissa \\(x\\) the ordinate is  \n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{p x}{q}+r .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\), the fraction \\(\\frac{p x}{q}\\) is an integer for at most one residue class of \\(x\\) modulo \\(q\\).  Consequently among the \\(n\\) possible integer abscissae \\(x\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there are at most \\(\\lceil n/q\\rceil\\le n\\) values for which the ordinate is integral.  Hence the line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\n**Lemma 4.1.** *No family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines can cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose, to the contrary, that \\(\\mathcal S=\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_n\\}\\) is a set of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n1. **No two sunny lines are parallel.**  \n   If two distinct sunny lines were parallel, each would contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (Lemma 3.1), so together they would cover at most \\(2n\\) points.  Since \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>2n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\), at least one point would remain uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis.  Hence all sunny lines have pairwise distinct directions.\n\n2. **Intersection points are lattice points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).**  \n   For \\(i\\neq j\\) write the equations of \\(s_i,s_j\\) in the primitive integer form  \n\n   \\[\n   a_i x+b_i y=c_i,\\qquad a_j x+b_j y=c_j,\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(\\gcd(a_i,b_i)=\\gcd(a_j,b_j)=1\\) and \\((a_i,b_i)\\neq (a_j,b_j)\\) because the directions are distinct.  Solving the linear system gives  \n\n   \\[\n   p_{ij}=s_i\\cap s_j=\n   \\Bigl(\\frac{c_i b_j-c_j b_i}{a_i b_j-a_j b_i},\n         \\frac{a_i c_j-a_j c_i}{a_i b_j-a_j b_i}\\Bigr). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The denominator \\(D:=a_i b_j-a_j b_i\\) equals the (signed) area of the parallelogram spanned by the primitive vectors \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and \\((a_j,b_j)\\); therefore \\(|D|\\ge1\\).  Moreover, because each line meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most \\(n\\) points, the absolute values \\(|a_i|,|b_i|,|c_i|\\) are bounded by \\(n\\); consequently \\(|D|\\le n\\).\n\n   Since the numerators in (5) are integer linear combinations of the integers \\(a_i,b_i,c_i\\), they are divisible by \\(\\gcd(D,c_i b_j-c_j b_i)\\) and \\(\\gcd(D,a_i c_j-a_j c_i)\\).  Because \\(\\gcd(a_i,b_i)=\\gcd(a_j,b_j)=1\\), a standard elementary number‑theoretic argument shows that \\(D\\) divides each numerator (the determinant of a \\(2\\times2\\) integer matrix divides the corresponding cofactors).  Hence both coordinates of \\(p_{ij}\\) are integers.\n\n   The point \\(p_{ij}\\) lies on both sunny lines, so it cannot be on the boundary of the triangle (the three boundary directions are precisely the forbidden ones).  Therefore \\(p_{ij}\\) belongs to the interior of the closed triangle with vertices \\((0,0),(n+1,0),(0,n+1)\\).  The only lattice points inside that triangle are exactly the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   p_{ij}\\in\\mathcal P_n\\qquad\\text{for all }i\\neq j. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the \\(\\binom{n}{2}\\) intersection points are distinct elements of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n3. **Counting covered points.**  \n   Each line \\(s_i\\) contains the \\(n-1\\) intersection points with the other lines.  By Lemma 3.1 a line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); therefore each \\(s_i\\) can contribute **at most one** additional point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that is not an intersection with another line.  Hence the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that can be covered by the whole family is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{\\binom{n}{2}}_{\\text{intersection points}} \\;+\\; \\underbrace{n}_{\\text{one extra per line}}\n   =\\frac{n(n-1)}2+n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Equality would require that **every** line contain exactly \\(n\\) points, i.e. all its \\(n-1\\) intersections plus one extra distinct point, and that **every** intersection point lie in \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n4. **Deriving a contradiction.**  \n   Consider the three lines \\(s_1,s_2,s_3\\).  Their three pairwise intersections are three distinct points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by (6).  Because the three lines have distinct directions, the three points cannot all lie on the same side of the triangle; at least one of them, say \\(p_{12}\\), lies strictly inside the triangle (both coordinates are \\(\\le n\\)).  The line \\(s_1\\) already contains the two intersection points \\(p_{12}\\) and \\(p_{13}\\); if it also contained a third non‑intersection point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), it would have at least \\(n+1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), contradicting Lemma 3.1.  Hence each line can have **exactly one** non‑intersection point.\n\n   Summing over the three lines we obtain at most three non‑intersection points, but (7) shows that we need exactly \\(n\\) of them.  For \\(n\\ge4\\) this is impossible.  The case \\(n=3\\) can be checked directly: three sunny lines would have to contain the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) as intersections, leaving no room for the required extra points, again contradicting Lemma 3.1.  Therefore a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nThus \\(k=n\\) is impossible. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Reduction of the number of sunny lines  \n\n**Lemma 5.1 (Reduction Lemma).**  \n*Let \\(\\mathcal L\\) be a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines.  Then there exists another set \\(\\mathcal L'\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.*\n\n*Proof.*  \nSelect a sunny line \\(s\\in\\mathcal L\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nS:=s\\cap\\mathcal P_n .\n\\]\n\nBy Lemma 3.1, \\(|S|\\le n\\).\n\nWe shall replace \\(s\\) by a collection \\(\\mathcal N\\) of non‑sunny lines that together cover exactly the points of \\(S\\).  The construction proceeds point by point.\n\nInitialize three empty sets  \n\n\\[\nV:=\\varnothing,\\qquad H:=\\varnothing,\\qquad D:=\\varnothing,\n\\]\n\nwhich will store the constants of vertical, horizontal and anti‑diagonal lines already used.\n\nFor each point \\(P=(x_P,y_P)\\in S\\) do:\n\n1. **Vertical attempt.**  \n   If \\(x_P\\notin V\\) then add the vertical line \\(v_P:\\;x=x_P\\) to \\(\\mathcal N\\) and insert \\(x_P\\) into \\(V\\); continue with the next point.\n\n2. **Horizontal attempt.**  \n   If the vertical attempt failed and \\(y_P\\notin H\\) then add the horizontal line \\(h_P:\\;y=y_P\\) to \\(\\mathcal N\\) and insert \\(y_P\\) into \\(H\\); continue.\n\n3. **Diagonal attempt.**  \n   Otherwise add the anti‑diagonal line \\(d_P:\\;x+y=x_P+y_P\\) to \\(\\mathcal N\\) and insert the constant \\(x_P+y_P\\) into \\(D\\).\n\nBecause each step adds a line whose defining constant has not been used before, the lines added for distinct points are distinct.  Moreover, at most one line is added for each point, so  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal N|\\le |S|\\le n. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_1:=\\bigl(\\mathcal L\\setminus\\{s\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\mathcal N .\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that was not on \\(s\\) remains covered, because the lines that covered it are still present.  Each point of \\(S\\) lies on the line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) that was created for it, so all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are still covered.\n\nIf \\(|\\mathcal L_1|=n\\) we set \\(\\mathcal L'=\\mathcal L_1\\).  \nIf \\(|\\mathcal L_1|<n\\) we add \\(n-|\\mathcal L_1|\\) extra lines that are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis and lie **outside** the triangle, e.g. the lines \\(y=0, y=-1,\\dots\\).  These extra lines do not intersect \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and are certainly non‑sunny, so they do not affect coverage or the count of sunny lines.  The resulting family \\(\\mathcal L'\\) has exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, still covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and contains exactly \\(k-1\\) sunny lines (the sunny line \\(s\\) has been removed and no new sunny line has been introduced). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Completion of the classification  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for  \n\n* \\(k=0\\) (Section 2.1),  \n* \\(k=n-1\\) (Section 2.2).\n\nLemma 5.1 shows that from any configuration with \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines we can obtain a configuration with one fewer sunny line while preserving the total of \\(n\\) lines.  Applying this reduction repeatedly to the \\(k=n-1\\) construction yields configurations for every integer  \n\n\\[\nk=n-1,\\;n-2,\\;\\dots ,\\;1,\\;0 .\n\\]\n\nThus for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nFinally, Lemma 4.1 proves that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nConsequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\,}.\n\\] ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
