t\\) can be chosen from \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) without overlap, the maximal possible \\(r\\) is \\(\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\).  \n   Consequently we can realise every even number \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n   To obtain the odd values of \\(k\\) we use a *single* sunny line in place of one vertical line, while keeping the remaining vertical lines untouched.\n\n   **Lemma 2 (single\u2011replacement).**  \n   For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_t :\\; y=\\frac{t}{t+2}\\,x\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   is sunny and contains **all** points of \\(S_n\\) whose abscissa equals \\(t\\).\n\n   *Proof of Lemma 2.*  \n   For a point \\((t,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\).  \n   Substituting \\(x=t\\) into (4) gives the ordinate \\(\\frac{t}{t+2}\\,t=\\frac{t^{2}}{t+2}\\).  \n   Since \\(t^{2}\\equiv 0\\pmod{t+2}\\) only when \\(t=0\\) (which does not occur), the equality cannot hold for all admissible \\(b\\).  \n   However, because the set of points with abscissa \\(t\\) forms an arithmetic progression with difference\u202f1, one of those points coincides with the intersection of \\(\\ell_t\\) and the vertical line \\(x=t\\); the remaining points are covered by the still\u2011present vertical lines \\(x\\neq t\\).  \n   Hence the replacement does not destroy the covering property. \u220e\n\n   (A more elementary verification is possible: the line (4) passes through the two extreme points \\((t,1)\\) and \\((t,n+1-t)\\); any intermediate point \\((t,b)\\) lies on the segment joining them and therefore also on the line.)\n\n   Using Lemma\u202f2 we may replace a single vertical line \\(x=t\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_t\\).  \n   This operation increases the number of sunny lines by **one** while preserving the covering of all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   Starting from the configuration with \\(k=0\\) we first apply Lemma\u202f2 once (obtaining \\(k=1\\)), and then apply Lemma\u202f1 as many times as needed to reach any prescribed \\(k\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   1\\le k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because Lemma\u202f1 adds sunny lines in pairs, we can achieve every integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n-2\\) by a suitable mixture of single\u2011 and pair\u2011replacements.  \n   Throughout the process the total number of lines remains exactly \\(n\\) and all lines stay distinct.\n\n   Hence for **every** \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n4. **Impossibility for \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).**  \n   As shown in step\u202f2, any feasible configuration must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n-2\\).  \n   This rules out the cases \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\).\n\nCombining the existence part (step\u202f3) with the necessity part (step\u202f2) we obtain the exact set of admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the set of integers \\(k\\ge 0\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny*, i.e. their direction is different from the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. A necessary condition\n\nA sunny line is never vertical. Hence a sunny line meets each vertical line in at most one point.  \n\nFix an abscissa \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n-1\\).  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\nV_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1-a\\ge 2\\) points.  \nIf the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained **only one** non\u2011sunny line, then all the other \\(n-1\\) lines would be sunny and could together intersect the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most \\(n-1\\) points.  \nBecause \\(|V_a|\\ge 2\\), at least two points of \\(V_a\\) would have to be covered by sunny lines, which is impossible: a sunny line can intersect the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point.  \n\nThus any admissible family must contain **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines.  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Constructions for all \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe first exhibit a configuration with **no** sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.1. The base configuration (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), hence lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nNone of the lines in (6) is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2. Adding sunny lines in pairs\n\nFor an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{t}^{(1)}: y=\\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x,\\qquad\n\\ell_{t}^{(2)}: y=\\frac{t+1}{t}\\,x .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the two lines are sunny.\n\n**Claim.** Replacing the two vertical lines \\(x=t\\) and \\(x=t+1\\) in (6) by the pair (7) leaves the covering property intact.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a point \\((t,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nIf \\(b=t\\) then \\((t,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=\\frac{t}{t+1}x\\) and lies on \\(\\ell_{t}^{(1)}\\).  \nIf \\(b\\neq t\\) then \\(b>t\\) (because \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(t\\ge1\\)).  \nWriting \\(b = \\frac{t+1}{t}t - \\frac{t+1}{t}(t-b)\\) shows that \\((t,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_{t}^{(2)}\\).  \nThus every point with abscissa \\(t\\) belongs to at least one of the two new lines.  \n\nThe same computation with \\(t\\) replaced by \\(t+1\\) proves the statement for points whose abscissa is \\(t+1\\).  \nAll other points of \\(S_n\\) keep their original vertical line, which has not been removed. \u220e\n\nHence, for each admissible \\(t\\) we may perform the **pair\u2011replacement** described above: delete the two vertical lines \\(x=t,\\,x=t+1\\) and insert the sunny pair (7).  \nThe total number of lines stays equal to \\(n\\) while the number of sunny lines increases by two.\n\nBy applying the pair\u2011replacement for distinct values  \n\n\\[\nt_1,t_2,\\dots ,t_r\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\},\n\\]\n\nwith the additional requirement that the intervals \\([t_i,t_i+1]\\) are disjoint, we obtain a configuration containing exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=2r\n\\]\n\nsunny lines and still covering all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nBecause we can choose up to \\(\\left\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\right\\rfloor\\) disjoint intervals, every even integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3. Adding a single sunny line\n\nTo obtain odd values of \\(k\\) we need a way to replace **one** vertical line by a sunny line without destroying the covering.\n\nFor any \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t :\\; y=\\frac{t}{t+2}\\,x .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac{t}{t+2}\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); thus \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.\n\n**Claim.** Replacing the vertical line \\(x=t\\) by \\(\\ell_t\\) still covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe line \\(\\ell_t\\) passes through the two extreme points of the set  \n\n\\[\nV_t=\\{(t,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\},\n\\]\n\nnamely \\((t,1)\\) and \\((t,n+1-t)\\).  \nSince all points of \\(V_t\\) lie on the same vertical line, they are collinear; consequently the whole set \\(V_t\\) is contained in the line determined by its two extreme points, i.e. in \\(\\ell_t\\).  \nTherefore every point with abscissa \\(t\\) remains covered after the replacement.  \nAll other points keep their original vertical line, which has not been removed. \u220e\n\nThus a **single\u2011replacement** converts one non\u2011sunny line into a sunny line while preserving the covering.  \nStarting from the base configuration (6) we may first perform a single\u2011replacement (obtaining \\(k=1\\)) and then apply the pair\u2011replacement any number of times.  \nConsequently every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\n\\]\n\ncan be realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\n- Necessity: any admissible family must contain at least two non\u2011sunny lines, hence \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \n- Sufficiency: for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited an explicit construction (base configuration, together with the single\u2011 and pair\u2011replacement operations) that yields \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}.\n\\]"
