ints.  \nBecause \\(|V_a|\\ge 2\\), at least two points of \\(V_a\\) would have to be covered by sunny lines, which is impossible: a sunny line can intersect the vertical line \\(x=a\\) in at most one point.  \n\nThus any admissible family must contain **at least two** non‑sunny lines.  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Constructions for all \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe first exhibit a configuration with **no** sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.1. The base configuration (\\(k=0\\))\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), hence lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nNone of the lines in (6) is sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2. Adding sunny lines in pairs\n\nFor an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-2\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{t}^{(1)}: y=\\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x,\\qquad\n\\ell_{t}^{(2)}: y=\\frac{t+1}{t}\\,x .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the two lines are sunny.\n\n**Claim.** Replacing the two vertical lines \\(x=t\\) and \\(x=t+1\\) in (6) by the pair (7) leaves the covering property intact.\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider a point \\((t,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nIf \\(b=t\\) then \\((t,b)\\) satisfies \\(y=\\frac{t}{t+1}x\\) and lies on \\(\\ell_{t}^{(1)}\\).  \nIf \\(b\\neq t\\) then \\(b>t\\) (because \\(b\\ge1\\) and \\(t\\ge1\\)).  \nWriting \\(b = \\frac{t+1}{t}t - \\frac{t+1}{t}(t-b)\\) shows that \\((t,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_{t}^{(2)}\\).  \nThus every point with abscissa \\(t\\) belongs to at least one of the two new lines.  \n\nThe same computation with \\(t\\) replaced by \\(t+1\\) proves the statement for points whose abscissa is \\(t+1\\).  \nAll other points of \\(S_n\\) keep their original vertical line, which has not been removed. ∎\n