S_n\\) that can lie on \\(\\ell\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\nm(\\ell)=\\min\\!\\left\\{\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p|}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n                     \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\right\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is sunny we have \\(|p|\\neq|q|\\) and both \\(|p|,|q|\\ge 1\\); therefore\n\\(\\min\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge 2\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nm(\\ell)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus **any sunny line contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Necessity of \\(k=0\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies condition (1)\nand contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Let the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines be\nnon\u2011sunny (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).  Denote by \\(|S_n|\\) the\ncardinality of \\(S_n\\); a simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nEach sunny line contributes at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points,\nwhile a non\u2011sunny line can cover any number of points (the trivial upper bound\n\\(|S_n|\\) is sufficient for our argument).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\n\\;\\le\\;\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\;+\\;(n-k)\\,|S_n|.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\((n-k)\\,|S_n|\\) from both sides yields\n\n\\[\n0\\;\\le\\;\nk\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-|S_n|\\Bigr).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n      >\\frac{n\\cdot n}{2}\n      \\ge \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\]\n\nso the factor in parentheses in (9) is **negative**.  The only way for (9) to hold\nis therefore  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain **no sunny line**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Sufficiency of \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach line of \\(\\mathcal V\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.\nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal V\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal V\\)\ncovers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nWe have proved that a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering\ncondition can exist only when \\(k=0\\), and we have exhibited an explicit family\nrealising \\(k=0\\).  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]"
