            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the possible numbers \\(k\\) of *sunny* lines in a family\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on\nat least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points that a sunny line can contain\n\nA line is sunny iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to\nthe line \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence its equation can be written in reduced integer form  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{p}{q}x+c ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwith \\((p,q)\\neq(0,1),(1,0),(-1,1)\\).\n\nIf \\((x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\) satisfies (1) then  \n\n\\[\nyq=px+cq .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(x\\) must satisfy a congruence modulo \\(|q|\\); the admissible\n\\(x\\)-coordinates form an arithmetic progression with step \\(|q|\\).  Because\nevery point of \\(S_n\\) has \\(1\\le x\\le n\\), the number of possible\n\\(x\\)-values on the line is at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/|q|\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\nThe same reasoning applied to the \\(y\\)-coordinates (step \\(|p|\\)) yields the\nbound \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/|p|\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\nConsequently a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) can contain at most  \n\n\\[\nm(\\ell)=\\min\\!\\Bigl\\{\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p|}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n                     \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  Since a sunny line is neither horizontal nor vertical nor\nof slope \\(-1\\), we have \\(|p|\\neq|q|\\) and \\(\\min\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge 2\\); therefore\n\n\\[\nm(\\ell)\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Points that a non‑sunny line can contain\n\nA non‑sunny line is of one of the three forms\n\n* vertical:   \\(x=a\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=b\\);\n* diagonal of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\),\n\nwith integer parameters.  For any such line the points of \\(S_n\\) it can contain\nare those with a fixed coordinate, and the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) shows that\nthere are at most \\(n\\) of them.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\text{any non‑sunny line contains }\\le n\\text{ points of }S_n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Necessity of \\(k=0\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the\ncovering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (so \\(n-k\\) lines\nare non‑sunny).  Using the bounds (4) and (5) we obtain an upper bound for the\ntotal number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered:\n\n\\[\n|S_n|\n\\;\\le\\;\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\;+\\;(n-k)\\,n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\), inequality (6) becomes\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (7) gives\n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-n\\Bigr)\n\\ge -\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor-n=-\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rceil\\le\n-\\frac{n}{2}\\), the left‑hand side of (8) is at most \\(-\\frac{n}{2}k\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{n}{2}k\\ge -\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nInequality (9) alone does not exclude sunny lines.  We now use a structural\nproperty of the set \\(S_n\\).\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on exactly one vertical line \\(x=a\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n\\), exactly one horizontal line \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), and\nexactly one diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  Thus the three\nfamilies  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=2,\\dots ,x+y=n+1\\}\n\\]\n\npartition \\(S_n\\) in the sense that each point belongs to one line of each\nfamily.\n\nA sunny line is **not** parallel to any line of \\(\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal H\\cup\n\\mathcal D\\); consequently a sunny line can intersect each of those families\nin at most one point.  Therefore a point of \\(S_n\\) that is covered **only**\nby sunny lines would have to be the unique intersection of a sunny line with\neach of the three families, which is impossible because a single line cannot\nsimultaneously intersect a vertical, a horizontal and a diagonal line at the\nsame point unless it coincides with one of them, contradicting sunniness.\n\nHence **every point of \\(S_n\\) must lie on at least one non‑sunny line**.\nSince there are only \\(n\\) lines in total, the number of non‑sunny lines is at\nleast the number of distinct families needed to cover all points, i.e. at least\n\\(1\\).  If \\(k\\ge 1\\) then the number of non‑sunny lines would be at most\n\\(n-1\\), contradicting the necessity that each of the \\(n\\) vertical (or\nhorizontal, or diagonal) lines required to reach every point appears among the\nfamily.  Therefore\n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Existence for \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V=\\{\\,x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), so\n\\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\) belonging to \\(\\mathcal V\\).  Thus \\(\\mathcal\nV\\) satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe argument above shows that any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all\npoints of \\(S_n\\) must contain no sunny line, and the explicit construction\ndemonstrates that this is achievable.  Consequently the set of admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
