on**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence it is **not** sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(H_b\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  From \\(k=0\\) to an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nt:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }t\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\nWe shall use  \n\n* the \\(t\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  H_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,t),\n  \\]\n\n  which are non‑sunny, and  \n\n* the \\(k\\) lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y = x + (t-i)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n  \\]\n\n  Since the slope \\(1\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(L_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\nAll the lines are distinct: the \\(H_j\\) have slope \\(0\\) while the \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); among the \\(L_i\\) the constants \\(t-i\\) are different, so the lines are different.\n\nWe now prove that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of these \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma  \n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) exactly one of the following holds  \n\n1. \\(b\\le t\\); then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).  \n2. \\(b>t\\); then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_{\\,n+1-a-b+t}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(b\\le t\\) the claim is immediate because \\(H_b\\) is one of our lines.\n\nAssume now