            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_j\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence it is **not** sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(H_b\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and set  \n\n\\[\nt:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }t\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\nWe shall use  \n\n* the \\(t\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  H_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,t),\n  \\]\n\n  which are non‑sunny, and  \n\n* the \\(k\\) lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y = x + (t-i+1)\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n  \\]\n\n  Because the slope \\(1\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(L_i\\) is **sunny**.\n\nAll these lines are distinct: the \\(H_j\\) have slope \\(0\\) while the \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\); among the \\(L_i\\) the constants \\(t-i+1\\) are different, so the lines are different.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma (covering)\n\nFor any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) exactly one of the following holds  \n\n1. **Case \\(b\\le t\\).**  \n   Then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n2. **Case \\(b>t\\).**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   i:=n+1-a-b+t+1.\n   \\]\n\n   Then \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\nIf \\(b\\le t\\) the claim is immediate because \\(H_b\\) belongs to our family.\n\nAssume now \\(b>t\\).  \nSince \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(a\\le n+1-b\\).  \nBecause \\(b\\ge t+1\\),\n\n\\[\ni=n+1-a-b+t+1\\ge n+1-(n+1-b)-b+t+1 = t+1\\ge 1,\n\\]\n\nand using \\(a\\ge 1\\),\n\n\\[\ni\\le n+1-1-b+t+1 = n-b+t+1\\le n-(t+1)+t+1 = n,\n\\]\n\nbut \\(k=n-t\\), so \\(i\\le k\\). Hence \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).\n\nNow evaluate the right‑hand side of the equation of \\(L_i\\) at \\(x=a\\):\n\n\\[\nx+(t-i+1)=a+\\bigl(t-(n+1-a-b+t+1)+1\\bigr)\n        =a+(a+b-n-1)=b.\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\); consequently it lies on \\(L_i\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBy the Lemma every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one of the lines\n\\(\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_t,L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\).  \nThe family contains exactly \\(t=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, i.e. precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nSince the family consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, no value of \\(k