 * **Case\u202f3.** \\(b>h\\) and \\(a>v\\).  \n     Set  \n     \\[\n     i:=a+ b - (h+v+1)=a+b-s-1 .\n     \\]\n     Because \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\) we have \\(a\\ge v+1\\) and \\(b\\ge h+1\\); consequently\n     \\[\n     1\\le i\\le k .\n     \\]\n     Moreover,\n     \\[\n     y = x + c_i\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b = a + (s+i)\n     \\]\n     and using the definition of \\(i\\) we obtain\n     \\[\n     a + (s+i)=a + s + a+b-s-1 = b .\n     \\]\n     Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\n   In every case the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.  \n\n4. **Counting.**  \n   The family consists of  \n\n   * \\(h\\) horizontal lines,\n   * \\(v\\) vertical lines,\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\),\n\n   altogether \\(h+v+k = s+k = n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\); together with the obvious bound \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) this yields the complete answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must find, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n* every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are sunny (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Necessary condition\n\nSince \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\nThus only the integers in this interval can possibly occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\ns:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non\u2011sunny lines we may use}).\n\\]\n\nSplit the non\u2011sunny lines into horizontal and vertical ones:\n\n\\[\nh:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{s}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad v:=s-h .\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,h),\\qquad \nV_j:\\; x=j\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,v).\n\\]\n\nAll \\(H_j\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis and all \\(V_j\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis; therefore none of them is sunny.\n\nFor the sunny lines we take \\(k\\) distinct lines of slope \\(1\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nc_i:=s+i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nand set  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + c_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nSince the slope \\(1\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe intercepts \\(c_i\\) are distinct, so the \\(L_i\\) are distinct as well.\n\nThe total family is  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_h\\}\\cup\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_v\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains exactly  \n\n\\[\nh+v+k = s+k = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering Lemma\n\nWe prove that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\na\\le n,\\qquad b\\le n .\n\\]\n\nConsider the three mutually exclusive possibilities.\n\n---\n\n#### Case\u202f1.  \\(b\\le h\\).\n\nThen the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (which exists because \\(1\\le b\\le h\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Case\u202f2.  \\(b>h\\) and \\(a\\le v\\).\n\nSince \\(1\\le a\\le v\\), the vertical line \\(V_a\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Case\u202f3.  \\(b>h\\) and \\(a>v\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni:=a+b-s-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a>v=s-h\\) we have \\(a\\ge s-h+1\\); because \\(b>h\\) we have \\(b\\ge h+1\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[