>h\\) and \\(a>v\\)\nThis is the crucial case where the point is not covered by any horizontal or vertical line, so the proof attempts to place it on a sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\n**Step 3.1 – Definition of \\(i\\)**\n> “Define \\(i:=a+b-s-1\\).”\n\n**Verification:** The definition is clear.\n\n**Step 3.2 – Showing \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)**\nThe solution argues:\n- From \\(a>v = s-h\\) we have \\(a\\ge s-h+1\\).\n- From \\(b>h\\) we have \\(b\\ge h+1\\).\n- Hence \\(i = a+b-s-1 \\ge (s-h+1)+(h+1)-s-1 = 1\\).\n\n- Using \\(a+b\\le n+1 = s+k+1\\) we get \\(i\\le k\\).\n\n**Verification:** The inequalities are algebraically correct; thus \\(i\\) is indeed an integer in \\([1,k]\\). So the index \\(i\\) corresponds to a defined sunny line \\(L_i\\).\n\n**Step 3.3 – Claim that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\)**\n> “Now compute the value of the right‑hand side of the equation of \\(L_i\\) at \\(x=a\\):\n\\[\na + c_i = a + (s+i) = a + s + (a+b-s-1) = b .\n\\]”\n\n**Critical Error Identification:**  \nThe algebraic manipulation is **incorrect**. Substituting \\(c_i = s+i\\) and \\(i = a+b-s-1\\) yields:\n\\[\na + c_i = a + s + i = a + s + (a+b-s-1) = 2a + b - 1,\n\\]\nnot \\(b\\). Therefore the point \\((a,b)\\) does **not** satisfy the equation \\(y = x + c_i\\) in general; it only does so when \\(2a - 1 = 0\\), i.e. \\(a = \\tfrac12\\), which is impossible for integer \\(a\\). Consequently the argument that every point in Case 3 lies on a sunny line fails.\n\nBecause the covering of points with \\(a>v\\) and \\(b>h\\) is essential (these are precisely the points not captured by the horizontal/vertical families), the construction does **not** guarantee that all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered. The claimed covering lemma is false.\n\n**Impact:** This error breaks the logical chain of the proof. Without a valid covering argument, the constructed family \\(\\mathcal L\\) does not satisfy the problem’s requirement for many values of \\(k\\). Hence the solution does not solve the problem