slopes of the \\(H_j\\) and \\(V_j\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\), respectively,\nso none of them is sunny.  The slope \\(1\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\);\nhence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the distinct intercepts \\(c_i\\) guarantee that\nthe \\(L_i\\) are pairwise distinct.  Altogether we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L| = h+v+k = s+k = n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Covering all points of \\(T_n\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Because \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1=s+k+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\na\\le s+k,\\qquad b\\le s+k .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWe distinguish three cases.\n\n#### Case 1.  \\(b\\le h\\).\n\nThen the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (which exists because \\(1\\le b\\le h\\))\ncontains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n#### Case 2.  \\(b>h\\) and \\(a\\le v\\).\n\nThen the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which exists because \\(1\\le a\\le v\\))\ncontains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n#### Case 3.  \\(b>h\\) and \\(a>v\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\ni:=b-a-s .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWe show that \\(i\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).\n\n*Upper bound.*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le s+k+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le s+k+1-a\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nb-a-s\\le k+1-2a\\le k ,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(a\\ge 1\\).  Hence \\(i\\le k\\).\n\n*Lower bound.*  \nSince \\(b>h\\) we have \\(b\\ge h+1\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le s+k+1\\) and the inequality \\(b\\ge h+1\\) we deduce  \n\n\\[\na\\le s+k+1-(h+1)=v+k .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\ni=b-a-s\\ge (h+1)-(v+k)-s\n      =h+1-v-k-(h+v)\n      =1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  By (3) we have \\(b=a+s+i\\), i.e.  \n\n\\[\nb = a + c_i .\n\\]\n\nTherefore \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(L_i\\); hence\n\\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).\n\nThe three cases are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, so every point of\n\\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of the family \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(T_n\\);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTogether with the necessary condition (1) this shows that the set of all\nadmissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
