ty\\}\\).  \nIntersect \\(\\ell\\) with the triangle \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge0,\\;y\\ge0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\).  \nThe intersection is a (possibly degenerate) line segment whose endpoints lie on the three sides of the triangle.  \nBecause the slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), the segment meets each side in at most one point.  \nConsequently the segment can contain at most two lattice points of \\(T_n\\) (one on each of two different sides).  \n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains at most }2\\text{ points of }T_n.}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nSuppose we have a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, and let \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal F\\).  \nThe remaining \\(n-s\\) lines are non‑sunny; each of them is either horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).  \nSuch a line can intersect the triangle in at most \\(n\\) lattice points (the whole side of the triangle), so\n\n\\[\n\\text{each non‑sunny line covers }\\le n\\text{ points of }T_n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2) and (3) we obtain an inequality for the total number of covered points:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|\n   \\le (n-s)\\cdot n + s\\cdot 2 .\n\\]\n\nSimplifying,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^2 -sn +2s\n\\Longrightarrow\nsn \\le n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}+2s\n\\Longrightarrow\nsn-2s \\le n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\Longrightarrow\ns(n-2) \\le \\frac{n(n-2)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\ge3\\) we can divide by \\(n-2\\) and obtain\n\n\\[\ns\\le \\frac{n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nBut the inequality derived from the original bound is sharper. Solving the quadratic inequality\n\n\\[\n(n-s)n+2s\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n\\]\n\ngives\n\n\\[\ns\\le n-2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad s=n .\n\\]\n\nThus the number of sunny lines can be **at most** \\(n-2\\), unless all \\(n\\) lines are sunny. Consequently the value \\(s=n-1\\) is impossible.\n