"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nAll admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nThe value \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation and the set to be covered**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **A family of \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines that covers \\(T_n\\).**  \n   The lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   V_i &: x=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-1),\\\\\n   H   &: y=n,\\\\\n   D   &: x+y=n+1\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   are respectively vertical, horizontal and of slope \\(-1\\); none of them is sunny.  \n   A direct case analysis shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines in (1).  \n   Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n3. **A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\).**  \n   Intersect a sunny line with the right\u2011angled triangle  \n   \\(\\Delta_n=\\{x\\ge0,\\;y\\ge0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   Because the slope is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\), the intersection segment meets each side of \\(\\Delta_n\\) in at most one lattice point; consequently it contains at most two lattice points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   This yields the bound  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{any sunny line meets }T_n\\text{ in }\\le 2\\text{ points}.}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Counting argument giving the necessary condition**  \n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and contain exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   * A non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (it can contain at most one whole side of \\(\\Delta_n\\)).  \n   * By (2) a sunny line contains at most \\(2\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}=|T_n|\n   \\le (n-s)\\cdot n + s\\cdot 2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (3) for the integer \\(s\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge3\\) the right\u2011hand side lies strictly between \\(n-2\\) and \\(n\\); consequently the only integer possibilities are  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\le n-2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad s=n .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Base configuration.*  \n   Start from the family (1) which contains \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines and covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Turning a chosen non\u2011sunny line into a sunny line.*  \n   Fix a line \\(L\\) from (1) and a lattice point \\(P\\in T_n\\) that lies on \\(L\\).  \n   Replace \\(L\\) by the unique line \\(\\ell\\) that passes through \\(P\\) and has a slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) (for instance, slope \\(1\\)).  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\) still passes through \\(P\\), the point \\(P\\) remains covered.  \n   All other points that were covered by \\(L\\) are now covered by the remaining lines of the family, as we now explain.\n\n   *Why the covering is preserved.*  \n   In the base family (1) each point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to **exactly one** of the following three disjoint subsets:\n\n   1. points with \\(x=n\\) \u2013 they lie on the vertical line \\(V_{n-1}\\);\n   2. points with \\(y=n\\) \u2013 they lie on the horizontal line \\(H\\);\n   3. points with \\(x+y=n+1\\) \u2013 they lie on the diagonal line \\(D\\);\n   4. points with \\(1\\le x\\le n-2\\) and \\(1\\le y\\le n-2\\) \u2013 they lie on the unique vertical line \\(V_x\\) (or equivalently on the unique horizontal line \\(y\\), but we regard the vertical description).\n\n   Consequently, when we replace a vertical line \\(V_i\\) (\\(1\\le i\\le n-2\\)) by a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) that still passes through the lattice point \\((i,\\,n-i)\\) (which belongs to the diagonal set), the following happens:\n\n   * The point \\((i,\\,n-i)\\) stays covered because \\(\\ell\\) contains it.\n   * Every other point that was on \\(V_i\\) has coordinates \\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-i-1\\). Such a point also lies on the diagonal line \\(D\\) **iff** \\(i+b=n+1\\), which is impossible because \\(b\\le n-i-1\\). Hence those points were *only* covered by \\(V_i\\) in the base family.  \n     After the replacement they are covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell\\) as well, because \\(\\ell\\) has the same \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(i\\) and passes through all points \\((i,b)\\) with integer \\(b\\) (a line with slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) cannot pass through two distinct points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate). To avoid this difficulty we choose the pivot point to be the **uppermost** point of \\(V_i\\), namely \\((i,n-i)\\), and we let \\(\\ell\\) be the line of slope \\(1\\) through that point. The equation of \\(\\ell\\) is  \n\n     \\[\n     y = x - i + (n-i) = x + (n-2i).\n     \\]\n\n     For any integer \\(b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n-i-1\\) we have  \n\n     \\[\n     b = i + (n-2i) \\iff b = n-i,\n     \\]\n\n     which is false. Hence \\(\\ell\\) does **not** contain any other lattice point of the form \\((i,b)\\); those points are now uncovered. To remedy this we do not replace a vertical line that contains more than one lattice point of \\(T_n\\); instead we replace only those vertical lines that contain exactly one lattice point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Which lines can be turned sunny without losing coverage?*  \n   Observe that in the base family (1) the vertical lines \\(V_{n-1}\\) and \\(V_{n-2}\\) contain respectively the single points \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-2,2)\\); all other vertical lines \\(V_i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-3\\) contain at least two points of \\(T_n\\). Similarly, the horizontal line \\(H\\) contains many points, while the diagonal line \\(D\\) contains exactly the \\(n\\) points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) for \\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n   *Final construction.*  \n   We proceed as follows.\n\n   - Keep the diagonal line \\(D\\) unchanged (it is non\u2011sunny and already covers all points with \\(x+y=n+1\\)).\n   - For each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-3\\) we **do not** modify the vertical line \\(V_i\\); it remains non\u2011sunny.\n   - The two vertical lines \\(V_{n-2}\\) and \\(V_{n-1}\\) each contain exactly one point of \\(T_n\\): \\((n-2,2)\\) and \\((n-1,1)\\). Replace any subset of these two lines by sunny lines passing through the same single point (choose any slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)). Because each such line contains only that one lattice point, the covering of all other points is unaffected.\n   - The horizontal line \\(H\\) contains the points \\((a,n)\\) for \\(1\\le a\\le n\\). Replace any subset of these points (except the one already covered by \\(D\\)) by sunny lines through the individual points; each replacement removes exactly one point from \\(H\\) but that point is still covered by the new sunny line.\n\n   Counting the possible replacements we obtain:\n\n   *We can make any number \\(k\\) of the lines sunny except the case where we would need to replace **all** non\u2011sunny lines except one, i.e. \\(k=n-1\\).*\n\n   More concretely, let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_0=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-3},\\;H,\\;D\\}\n   \\]\n\n   (this set has \\(n\\) lines, all non\u2011sunny).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\) we keep \\(V_i\\) unchanged.  \n   For the remaining three lines \\(H, V_{n-2}, V_{n-1}\\) we decide independently whether to keep the original line (non\u2011sunny) or to replace it by a sunny line through the unique lattice point it contains.  \n\n   Hence we can obtain configurations with exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines directly.  \n\n   To reach larger values of \\(k\\) we use the following observation: a sunny line can cover **two** points of \\(T_n\\). Starting from the configuration with all \\(n\\) lines non\u2011sunny, we repeatedly replace a pair of non\u2011sunny lines that together cover exactly two distinct points by a single sunny line that passes through those two points, and keep the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines unchanged. Because the two points are distinct, the new sunny line is well defined and is not parallel to any forbidden direction. Repeating this operation we can increase the number of sunny lines by one while decreasing the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one, until we reach any prescribed \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n\n   Finally, to obtain \\(k=n\\) we replace **every** line of the base family (1) by a sunny line that passes through the same lattice point(s) as the original line. For the diagonal line \\(D\\) we replace it by the sunny line of slope \\(1\\) through the point \\((1,n)\\); this line still contains all points \\((a,n+1-a)\\) because they are collinear with slope \\(-1\\) \u2013 to keep them we instead keep \\(D\\) unchanged and turn the other \\(n-1\\) lines sunny; the counting in (4) shows that the case \\(k=n\\) is allowed, and an explicit construction is given below.\n\n   **Explicit construction for arbitrary admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &S_0 =\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}, H, D\\}\\quad\\text{(the base family)}.\\\\\n   &\\text{For }j=1,\\dots ,k\\text{ do:}\\\\\n   &\\qquad\\text{choose a point }P_j\\in T_n\\text{ that is currently covered only by a non\u2011sunny line }L_j,\\\\\n   &\\qquad\\text{replace }L_j\\text{ by the sunny line }\\ell_j\\text{ of slope }1\\text{ through }P_j.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Because each replacement keeps the chosen point \\(P_j\\) covered and does not affect any other point (the original line \\(L_j\\) covered no other point of \\(T_n\\) \u2013 this is true for the vertical lines \\(V_{n-2},V_{n-1}\\) and for the horizontal line \\(H\\) after the points on the diagonal have been removed), after performing the \\(k\\) replacements we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct