  *Proof.*  \n   The triangle \\(\\Delta_n=\\{(x,y)\\mid x\\ge0,\\;y\\ge0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\) has vertices \\((0,0),(n+1,0),(0,n+1)\\).  \n   A sunny line meets the three sides of \\(\\Delta_n\\) in three distinct points (otherwise it would be parallel to one side).  \n   Any lattice point of \\(T_n\\) lies on the interior of \\(\\Delta_n\\) or on one of its sides.  \n   Because the slope of the line is not \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\), the line can intersect each side in **at most one** lattice point (the side is a lattice segment of slope \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) or \\(\\infty\\)).  \n   Hence the line can contain at most one lattice point on each side, i.e. at most three lattice points in total.  \n   If it contained three, two of them would belong to the same side of the triangle, contradicting the previous observation.  \n   Therefore a sunny line contains at most two points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n   *Lemma 2 (non‑sunny lines).*  \n   Any non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains **at most \\(n\\)** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   A vertical line \\(x=c\\) meets the inequality \\(c+b\\le n+1\\) with \\(b\\ge1\\); thus \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\).  \n   Since \\(c\\ge1\\), we have \\(n+1-c\\le n\\). The same bound holds for a horizontal line.  \n   A line of slope \\(-1\\) is precisely the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\); it contains exactly the \\(n\\) points \\((a,n+1-a)\\;(1\\le a\\le n)\\). ∎\n\n4. **A necessary condition on the number of sunny lines**  \n\n   Let a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering requirement and contain exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines.  \n   Split \\(\\mathcal F\\) into the set \\(\\mathcal S\\) of sunny lines (\\(|\\mathcal S|=s\\)) and the set \\(\\mathcal N\\) of non‑sunny lines (\\(|\\mathcal N|=n-s\\)).  \n   By Lemma 1 each line of \\(\\mathcal S\\) covers at most \\(2\\) points of \\(T_n\\); by Lemma 2 each line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) covers at most \\(n\\) points.  \n   Consequently\n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|\n   \\le (n-s)\\cdot n + s\\cdot 2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le n^2- ns +2s\n   \\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n   s\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)} .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n\\ge3\\) the right–hand side lies strictly between \\(n-2\\) and \\(n\\); therefore the only integer possibilities are  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\le n-2\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad s=n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)**  \n\n   *Case \\(k=0\\).*  \n   The base family \\(\\mathcal F_{\\text{base}}\\) (2) already provides a configuration with no sunny lines.\n\n   *Case \\(k=n\\).*  \n   For each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{a,b}:\\; y = x + (b-a) .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); hence every \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\) is sunny.  \n   Distinct points give distinct intercepts \\(b-a\\), so the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{\\ell_{a,b}\\mid (a,b)\\in T_n\\}\\) are pairwise distinct.  \n   Obviously each point \\((a,b)\\) lies on its own line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\); therefore the family of the \\(n\\) sunny lines covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Case \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).*  \n   We start from the base family \\(\\mathcal F_{\\text{base}}\\) and turn exactly \\(k\\) of its vertical lines into sunny lines, leaving the diagonal line \\(D\\) unchanged.  \n   The vertical lines that we are allowed to replace are the last \\(k\\) of them, namely  \n\n   \\[\n   V_{n-k},V_{n-k+1},\\dots ,V_{n-1}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\) in this range we construct a sunny line \\(\\sigma_i\\) that passes through the **unique** point of \\(V_i\\) which is **not** on the diagonal \\(D\\).  \n   That point is \\((i,\\,n+1-i-1)=(i,\\,n-i)\\).  \n   Choose any slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) that is not already used; for definiteness we take slope \\(2\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_i:\\; y- (n-i)=2\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)\n   \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n   y = 2x -i + (n-2i).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\sigma_i\\) is sunny by construction and contains the point \\((i,n-i)\\).  \n   The point \\((i,n-i)\\) was the only point of \\(V_i\\) that was **not** already covered by the diagonal \\(D\\); therefore after removing \\(V_i\\) and inserting \\(\\sigma_i\\) the covering of \\(T_n\\) is unchanged.\n\n   It remains to check that the new sunny lines are distinct from each other and from the lines that stay unchanged.  \n   Distinct indices \\(i\\neq j\\) give different intercepts in (8), so \\(\\sigma_i\\neq\\sigma_j\\).  \n   Moreover none of the \\(\\sigma_i\\) coincides with the diagonal \\(D\\) (their slopes are \\(2\\neq -1\\)) nor with any vertical line that remains in the family.  \n\n   Consequently the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\n   \\bigl\\{\\,V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k-1}\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\,\\sigma_{n-k},\\dots ,\\sigma_{\\,n-1}\\,\\bigr\\}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\{D\\}\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(\\sigma_{n-k},\\dots ,\\sigma_{n-1}\\)), and still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\n   Since \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n-2\\), we have exhibited a construction for every admissible value.\n\n6. **Conclusion**  \n\n   The counting argument (5) shows that the number of sunny lines can only be \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\) or \\(n\\).  \n   The explicit families (2), (9) and (6) demonstrate that each of these numbers indeed occurs, while the case \\(k=n-1\\) is ruled out by (5).  \n\n   Hence the complete answer is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{All }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\\text{ are possible; }k=n-1\\text{ is impossible}.}\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and th