e sunny line \\(x+y=n+1-i\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).\n   - Keep all other vertical lines and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n   The resulting family consists of:\n   * \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(x+y=n+1-i\\) (\\(i\\in I\\));\n   * \\((n-2)-k\\) untouched vertical lines;\n   * the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n   All lines are distinct because the equations \\(x=i\\) and \\(x+y=n+1-i\\) are different for each \\(i\\). Moreover, each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies either on its vertical line \\(x=a\\) (if \\(a\\notin I\\)), on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (if \\(b=1\\)), or on the sunny line \\(x+y=n+1-a\\) (if \\(a\\in I\\)). Hence the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n   Consequently, for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the upper bound (1) with the explicit constructions, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}}.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem requires a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n\n1. every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to any of the three directions \\(x\\)-axis, \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound on \\(k\\)\n\nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) will be called *non‑sunny*.  \n\n**Lemma 1.** A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on a common non‑sunny line.\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a sunny line \\(L\\) contained three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\in T_n\\) that are collinear with a non‑sunny direction (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)). Then the line through any two of them would have that forbidden direction, forcing \\(L\\) to be parallel to it, contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎\n\nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\): the longest horizontal, vertical or diagonal segment inside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) has length \\(n\\).\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L\\) contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines. By Lemma 1 the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\); the non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points. Since \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+2k .\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n^2-nk+2k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nnk-2k\\le n^2-\\frac{n(n+1)}2=n\\Bigl(n-\\frac{n+1}2\\Bigr)=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(k\\) (or observing the inequality directly) yields \\(k\\le n-2\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.1. A base covering (all lines non‑sunny)\n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nL_n:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) has \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\); if \\(b=1\\) it lies on \\(L_n\\), otherwise it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\). Thus the \\(n\\) lines cover \\(T_n\\). All of them are non‑sunny, so the case \\(k=0\\) is realized.\n\n#### 2.2. Introducing sunny lines\n\nFix a subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in I\\) replace the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the line\n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;x+y=n+1-i .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is sunny (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) that satisfy \\(x+y=n+1-i\\) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,\\,n+1-i),\\;(i+1,\\,n-i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nHence each sunny line \\(S_i\\) covers precisely the two points of \\(T_n\\) whose abscissa equals \\(i\\) or \\(i+1\\).\n\nAll other vertical lines \\(x=j\\) with \\(j\\notin I\\) are kept unchanged, and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) is also kept. Consequently the final family is\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\n\\bigl\\{\\,S_i\\mid i\\in I\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{\\,x=j\\mid j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus I\\,\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,y=1\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n|I|+(n-1-|I|)+1=n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(|I|=k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n#### 2.3. Verification of the covering\n\nTake any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n* If \\(b>1\\) then \\(a\\le n-1\\).  \n  – If \\(a\\in I\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b=n+1-a\\) or \\(a+b=n-a\\); in either case \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of the sunny line \\(S_a\\) and is covered.  \n  – If \\(a\\notin I\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and covers the point.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nAll lines are distinct: the equations \\(x=j\\) (\\(j\\notin I\\)), \\(y=1\\), and \\(x+y=n+1-i\\) (\\(i\\in I\\)) are pairwise different.\n\nHence for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have constructed a suitable family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\) follows from a counting argument, and the explicit construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can indeed be attained.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The possible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2.}\n\\]"
