 ,x=n-1\\) together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) either has \\(b=1\\) (hence lies on \\(y=1\\)) or \\(b>1\\) and then lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\). All these lines are non\u2011sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n   *Introducing sunny lines.*  \n   Choose any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\in I\\) **replace** the vertical line \\(x=i\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y = \\frac{1}{2}\\,x +\\frac{2i+1-n}{2}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_i\\) is \\(\\tfrac12\\); therefore it is sunny (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   The two lattice points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(S_i\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (i,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (i+1,2).\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, substituting \\((i,1)\\) into (2) gives \\(1=\\tfrac12 i+\\frac{2i+1-n}{2}\\), which simplifies to \\(i+1=n\\); because \\(i\\le n-2\\) this equality holds, and similarly for \\((i+1,2)\\). Consequently each sunny line covers exactly the two points of the column \\(x=i\\) that are not covered by any other line.\n\n   After the replacement, the set of lines consists of  \n\n   * the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\);  \n   * the untouched vertical lines \\(x=j\\) for \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus I\\);  \n   * the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).\n\n   The total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k+\\bigl((n-1)-k\\bigr)+1=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification of the covering.*  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   * If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   * If \\(b>1\\) and \\(a\\notin I\\) then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which is present) contains the point.  \n   * If \\(b>1\\) and \\(a\\in I\\) then necessarily \\(b=2\\) (because the only points of column \\(x=a\\) not on \\(y=1\\) are \\((a,2)\\) and \\((a,3)\\), but \\((a,3)\\) would violate \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) for \\(a\\le n-2\\)).