on it.\n\n   3. **If \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\in I\\)**, then \\(a\\le n-2\\).  \n      Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-1\\).  \n      The only points of the column \\(x=a\\) that are *not* already covered by \\(H\\) are those with \\(b\\ge2\\).  \n      Among those, the point \\((a,2)\\) is covered by the sunny line \\(S_a\\) because of (3).  \n      For any larger \\(b\\) (\\(b\\ge3\\)) we note that the point \\((a,b)\\) also belongs to the vertical line \\(V_{a+1}\\): indeed \\(a+1\\le n-1\\) and\n      \\[\n      (a+1)+(b-1)=a+b\\le n+1 .\n      \\]\n      Hence \\((a,b)=(a+1,b-1)\\) lies on \\(V_{a+1}\\), which is present unless \\(a+1\\in I\\).  \n      If \\(a+1\\in I\\) we repeat the argument one step further; because the set \\(I\\) contains at most \\(k\\le n-2\\) indices, after at most two steps we reach a vertical line that has not been replaced (the index \\(n-1\\) is never in \\(I\\)). Consequently every point with \\(b\\ge3\\) is covered by some vertical line \\(V_{a'}\\) with \\(a'\\notin I\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly the lines \\(\\{S_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\) are sunny. Since the choice of \\(I\\) is arbitrary, we obtain a valid configuration for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (1) together with the explicit constructions shows that the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]"

