            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside (and on) the right‑isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIts cardinality is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\nAll lines in the sequel are assumed to be distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  Lemma 1 – Sunny lines meet at most two points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line. Write it in the form \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\neq0,-1\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) contains two distinct lattice points \\((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\\in T_n\\) then  \n\n\\[\ny_2-y_1=m(x_2-x_1).\n\\]\n\nHence \\(m\\) is rational; write \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).  \nFrom the above equality we obtain  \n\n\\[\nq\\mid (x_2-x_1),\\qquad p\\mid (y_2-y_1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAssume that a third point \\((x_3,y_3)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(L\\).  \nApplying (2) to the pairs \\((x_1,y_1),(x_3,y_3)\\) and \\((x_2,y_2),(x_3,y_3)\\) shows that the differences\n\\(|x_3-x_1|\\) and \\(|x_3-x_2|\\) are both multiples of \\(q\\); consequently the smallest and the largest\n\\(x\\)-coordinates among the three points differ by at least \\(2q\\).  \nThe corresponding \\(y\\)-coordinates differ by at least \\(2|p|\\).\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl[(x_{\\max}+y_{\\max})-(x_{\\min}+y_{\\min})\\bigr]\n   \\ge 2(q+|p|)\\ge 4 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\); the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) has length \\(n-1\\).  \nIf three points of \\(T_n\\) lay on \\(L\\), their values of \\(x+y\\) would have to differ by at least \\(4\\) by (3), which is impossible when \\(n\\ge3\\).  \nHence a sunny line meets at most two points of \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Lemma 2 – Non‑sunny lines meet at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line has equation \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\).  \nInside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); thus the line contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\le n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nA vertical line \\(x=a\\) (\\(1\\le a\\le n\\)) contains the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\); again at most \\(n\\) points.\n\nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\).  \nInside the triangle we must have \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); for each admissible \\(c\\) the integer solutions of \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(x,y\\ge1\\) are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\), i.e. exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a configuration satisfying the statement of the problem contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\n*Claim.*  At least **two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nThe points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nIf a line is sunny, its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\); consequently a sunny line cannot contain two points that have the same \\(y\\)-coordinate (otherwise the slope would be \\(0\\)) nor two points that have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate (otherwise the slope would be \\(\\infty\\)).  \nHence the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) must lie on **different** lines, and each of those lines must be non‑sunny (horizontal or vertical).  \nThus at least two non‑sunny lines are present. ∎\n\nNow count how many points of \\(T_n\\) can be covered.\n\n* By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points, so the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n* By Lemma 2 each non‑sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points, so the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.\n\nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, we obtain the inequality  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\;=\\;|T_n|\n   \\;\\le\\;(n-k)n+2k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (4) gives  \n\n\\[\nk(n-2)\\;\\le\\;n(n-2)/2 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge3\\) we may divide by the positive factor \\(n-2\\) and obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\;\\le\\; \\frac{n}{2}\\; .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBut we have already proved that at least two lines are non‑sunny; therefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nInequality (6) is the desired upper bound.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose an arbitrary subset  \n\n\\[\nI\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\},\\qquad |I|=k .\n\\]\n\nDefine the following family of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n* **Horizontal line**  \n  \\[\n  H:\\;y=1 .\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n* **Vertical lines** (those whose indices are *not* in \\(I\\))  \n  \\[\n  V_j:\\;x=j ,\\qquad j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus I .\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\n* **Sunny lines** (replacements for the indices in \\(I\\))  \n  \\[\n  S_i:\\;y = x-i+1 ,\\qquad i\\in I .\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(S_i\\) equals \\(1\\); it is therefore sunny because the only forbidden slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nThe family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the horizontal line \\(H\\) and the vertical lines \\(V_j\\)).  \nAll lines are distinct because the sets of their slopes are disjoint.\n\nWe now verify that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines.\n\n* **Case 1.** \\(b=1\\).  \n  Then \\((a,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\).\n\n* **Case 2.** \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\notin I\\).  \n  The vertical line \\(V_a\\) is present, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\n* **Case 3.** \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\in I\\).  \n  Because \\(a\\le n-2\\) (by the definition of \\(I\\)), the sunny line \\(S_a\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n  \\[\n  (a,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (a+1,2)\n  \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n\n  (substituting \\(x=a\\) and \\(x=a+1\\) into (9) gives \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=2\\) respectively).  \n  Hence the point \\((a,2)\\) is covered by \\(S_a\\).\n\n  For any larger \\(b\\) (\\(b\\ge3\\)) we use the following observation:  \n\n  \\[\n  (a,b) = (a+1,b-1) .\n  \\tag{11}\n  \\]\n\n  Since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have \\((a+1)+(b-1)=a+b\\le n+1\\); thus \\((a+1,b-1)\\) also belongs to \\(T_n\\).  \n  If \\(a+1\\notin I\\) then the vertical line \\(V_{a+1}\\) is present and contains \\((a+1,b-1)=(a,b)\\).  \n  If \\(a+1\\in I\\) we repeat the shift (11) once more.  \n  Because the index \\(n-1\\) never belongs to \\(I\\) (recall \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\)), after at most two repetitions we reach a column whose index is not in \\(I\\); the corresponding vertical line is present and covers the point.  \n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one line of the family.\n\nConsequently, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma 1 and Lemma 2 give the inequality (4), which together with the observation that at least two lines must be non‑sunny yields the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThe explicit construction of Section 5 shows that this bound is also sufficient.  \nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
