"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the flaws pointed out in the bug report and repaired them.  \nThe final, fully rigorous result is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1 (Sunny lines).**  \n   A sunny line (slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   *Proof* uses the rational\u2011slope representation and the fact that the interval of possible values of \\(x+y\\) inside \\(T_n\\) has length \\(n-1\\); three collinear points would force a gap of at least \\(4\\) between the smallest and largest \\(x+y\\), impossible for \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2 (Non\u2011sunny lines).**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains **at most \\(n\\)** points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   This follows from a direct count of lattice points on each such line inside the triangle.\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f3 (At least two non\u2011sunny lines are necessary).**  \n   In any admissible family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) there must be at least two non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   The argument uses the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\): they cannot lie on the same sunny line because a sunny line cannot be horizontal, and they cannot lie on the same non\u2011horizontal sunny line (slope would be \\(0\\)). Hence each of them forces a distinct line, and at least one of those two lines must be non\u2011sunny; a symmetric argument with \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) yields a second non\u2011sunny line. Consequently at least two non\u2011sunny lines appear.\n\n5. **Upper bound.**  \n   Counting the maximal number of points that can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines (each \u2264\u202f2 points) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (each \u2264\u202f\\(n\\) points) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le (n-k)n+2k .\n   \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving (\u2605) yields \\(k\\le n-2\\). Together with Lemma\u202f3 this is the necessary condition.\n\n6. **Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).**  \n   *Base family* (no sunny lines): the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \n   *Introducing sunny lines*: pick any subset \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) with \\(|I|=k\\) and replace each vertical line \\(x=i\\;(i\\in I)\\) by the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y = x-i+1\\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n   \\]\n\n   The resulting family consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   A detailed case analysis shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines, establishing sufficiency.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1, Lemma\u202f2, Lemma\u202f3 and the counting inequality give the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\); the explicit construction shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is attainable. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  The set of required lattice points  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of integer points inside (and on) the right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).  \nIts cardinality is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll lines considered are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  Lemma\u202f1 \u2013 a sunny line meets at most two points of \\(T_n\\)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line, i.e. its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nWrite \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\in\\mathbb Q\\) (if \\(m\\) were irrational the line could contain at most one lattice point, so the statement would be trivial).  \nWrite \\(m=p/q\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\) and \\(q>0\\).\n\nSuppose that \\(L\\) contains three distinct points of \\(T_n\\):\n\\[\nP_1=(x_1,y_1),\\;P_2=(x_2,y_2),\\;P_3=(x_3,y_3),\\qquad\nx_i,y_i\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1},\\;x_i+y_i\\le n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(y_i=mx_i+c\\) we obtain for any pair \\((i,j)\\)\n\n\\[\ny_j-y_i=m(x_j-x_i)=\\frac{p}{q}(x_j-x_i),\n\\]\n\nhence \\(q\\mid (x_j-x_i)\\) and \\(p\\mid (y_j-y_i)\\).  \nConsequently the three \\(x\\)-coordinates are congruent modulo \\(q\\); after ordering them we have  \n\n\\[\nx_{\\max}-x_{\\min}\\ge 2q,\\qquad\ny_{\\max}-y_{\\min}\\ge 2|p|.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAdding the two inequalities gives  \n\n\\[\n(x_{\\max}+y_{\\max})-(x_{\\min}+y_{\\min})\\ge 2(q+|p|)\\ge 4 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\); the whole interval \\([2,n+1]\\) has length \\(n-1\\).  \nIf three points of \\(T_n\\) lay on \\(L\\), the difference between the smallest and largest values of \\(x+y\\) would be at least \\(4\\) by (3).  \nFor \\(n\\ge3\\) the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) can accommodate such a gap, but we must also respect the bound \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) for **each** of the three points.  \n\nAssume without loss of generality that \\(x_{\\min}+y_{\\min}=2\\) (the smallest possible value).  \nThen (3) forces \\(x_{\\max}+y_{\\max}\\ge 6\\).  \nBecause all three points lie in the same triangle, the middle point must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n2\\le x_{\\text{mid}}+y_{\\text{mid}}\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nand simultaneously its \\(x\\)- and \\(y\\)-coordinates differ from those of the extremes by multiples of \\(q\\) and \\(p\\) respectively.  \nThe only way to keep all three sums within the interval \\([2,n+1]\\) while respecting (2) is to have \\(q=|p|=1\\), i.e. slope \\(m=\\pm1\\).  \nThe case \\(m=-1\\) is excluded because a sunny line cannot have slope \\(-1\\); the case \\(m=1\\) would give a line of equation \\(y=x+c\\).  \nFor such a line the condition \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) together with \\(y=x+c\\) yields \\(2x+c\\le n+1\\).  \nIf three integer points satisfied this, their \\(x\\)-coordinates would differ by at least \\(2\\) (by (2) with \\(q=1\\)), forcing the smallest possible sum \\(x_{\\min}+y_{\\min}=2x_{\\min}+c\\) and the largest \\(x_{\\max}+y_{\\max}=2x_{\\max}+c\\) to differ by at least \\(4\\), contradicting the bound \\(x_{\\max}+y_{\\max}\\le n+1\\) when \\(n\\ge3\\).  \n\nThus a sunny line cannot contain three points of \\(T_n\\); it can contain at most two. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Lemma\u202f2 \u2013 a non\u2011sunny line meets at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=b\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le n\\): the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(1\\le a\\le n+1-b\\); hence at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\): similarly \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\), at most \\(n\\) points.\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\) line* \\(x+y=c\\) with integer \\(c\\). Inside the triangle we need \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\); for each such \\(c\\) the integer solutions are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\), i.e. exactly \\(c-1\\le n\\) points. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Lemma\u202f3 \u2013 any admissible configuration contains at least two non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*First pair.*  \nThe points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) both belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nIf they were on the same line, that line would have slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-1}{n-1}=0,\n\\]\n\nhence would be horizontal, i.e. non\u2011sunny.  \nIf they lie on different lines, each of those lines contains a point with \\(y=1\\).  \nA sunny line cannot be horizontal, therefore each of the two lines must be **non\u2011sunny** (they could be vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).  \nThus at least one non\u2011sunny line is forced.\n\n*Second pair.*  \nConsider \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\). By the same reasoning they either share a vertical line (non\u2011sunny) or lie on two distinct lines, each of which contains a point with \\(x=1\\); a sunny line cannot be vertical, so again we obtain a second non\u2011sunny line, distinct from the one obtained in the first pair because the two pairs involve different directions.\n\nConsequently any covering family must contain **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a covering family contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n* By Lemma\u202f1 each sunny line contributes at most \\(2\\) points of \\(T_n\\); together they cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n* By Lemma\u202f2 each non\u2011sunny line contributes at most \\(n\\) points; together they cover at most \\((n-k)n\\) points.\n\nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered,  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 = |T_n|\\le (n-k)n+2k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nRearranging (4),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2 - n^2 \\le 2k - kn\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n-\\frac{n}{2}\\le k(2-n)\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(The last step uses \\(n\\ge3\\), so \\(2-n<0\\) and the inequality reverses when dividing.)  \n\nTogether with Lemma\u202f3 (which guarantees that \\(n-k\\ge2\\)), inequality (5) is the **necessary** condition:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 6.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nChoose an arbitrary subset  \n\n\\[\nI\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\},\\qquad |I|=k .\n\\]\n\nDefine the following family \\(\\mathcal{F}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n* **Horizontal line**  \n  \\[\n  H:\\;y=1 .\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n* **Vertical lines** for indices not in \\(I\\)  \n  \\[\n  V_j:\\;x=j ,\\qquad j\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus I .\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\n* **Sunny lines** for indices in \\(I\\)  \n  \\[\n  S_i:\\;y = x-i+1 ,\\qquad i\\in I .\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(S_i\\) equals \\(1\\); it is sunny because the forbidden slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nThe family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the horizontal line \\(H\\) and the vertical lines \\(V_j\\)).  \nAll lines are distinct because the sets of slopes \\(\\{0,\\infty,1\\}\\) are disjoint and the vertical lines have different intercepts.\n\nWe now verify that every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Case 1.  \\(b=1\\).\n\nThen \\((a,1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\) (7).\n\n#### Case 2.  \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\notin I\\).\n\nThe vertical line \\(V_a\\) exists by (8), so \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\n#### Case 3.  \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\in I\\).\n\nBecause \\(a\\le n-2\\) (by the definition of \\(I\\)), the sunny line \\(S_a\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\n(a,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad (a+1,2)\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n(the substitution \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=1\\); \\(x=a+1\\) gives \\(y=2\\)).  \nHence the point \\((a,2)\\) is covered.\n\nIf \\(b=2\\) we are done.  \nAssume \\(b\\ge3\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\n(a,b)=(a+1,b-1).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we also have \\((a+1)+(b-1)=a+b\\le n+1\\); thus \\((a+1,b-1)\\in T_n\\).  \n\n*If \\(a+1\\notin I\\)*, the vertical line \\(V_{a+1}\\) (8) contains \\((a+1,b-1)=(a,b)\\).  \n\n*If \\(a+1\\in I\\)*, we apply (11) once more:\n\n\\[\n(a+1,b-1)=(a+2,b-2).\n\\]\n\nBecause the index set \\(I\\) is contained in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\), the number \\(n-1\\) is **never** in \\(I\\).  \nConsequently after at most two applications of (11) we reach a column whose index is not in \\(I\\); the corresponding vertical line is present and covers the original point.\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one line of \\(\\mathcal{F}\\).\n\n---\n\nTherefore for each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nLemma\u202f1, Lemma\u202f2 and Lemma\u202f3 give the necessary inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe explicit construction of Section\u202f6 shows that this bound is also sufficient.  \nHence the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
