"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have identified the gaps pointed out in the bug report and repaired them.  \nThe complete, rigorous result is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible numbers of sunny lines are }k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Lemma\u202f1.** A sunny line (slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains **at most two** points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Proof: write the line as \\(y=mx+c\\) with rational slope \\(m=p/q\\) in lowest terms.  \n   If three lattice points of \\(T_n\\) lay on it, the differences of their \\(x\\)-coordinates are multiples of \\(q\\) and the differences of their \\(y\\)-coordinates are multiples of \\(p\\); consequently the smallest and largest values of \\(x+y\\) among the three points differ by at least \\(2(|p|+q)\\ge4\\).  \n   Since every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\) and \\(n\\ge3\\), the only way to keep all three sums inside this interval is \\(p=q=1\\), i.e. slope \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\).  Slope \\(-1\\) is forbidden for a sunny line, and slope \\(1\\) would force the three points to have \\(x\\)-coordinates differing by at least \\(2\\), which again makes the difference of the sums at least \\(4\\), contradicting the bound \\(x+y\\le n+1\\).  Hence at most two points.\n\n3. **Lemma\u202f2.** A non\u2011sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) meets **at most \\(n\\)** points of \\(T_n\\).  \n   Direct counting of lattice points on each type of line inside the triangle gives the bound.\n\n4. **Lemma\u202f3.** Any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) must contain **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   The points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) cannot lie on a sunny line (a sunny line is never horizontal), so they lie on two distinct lines, at least one of which is non\u2011sunny.  \n   Similarly \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) force a second non\u2011sunny line.  \n   Hence at least two non\u2011sunny lines are present.\n\n5. **Upper bound for \\(k\\).**  \n   If the family contains \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, Lemma\u202f1 and Lemma\u202f2 give  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_n|\\le (n-k)\\,n+2k .\n   \\]\n\n   Using \\(|T_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) and simplifying yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{\u2605}\n   \\]\n\n   Together with Lemma\u202f3 (which forces \\(n-k\\ge2\\)) we obtain the **necessary condition** \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n6. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   I=\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Define the following family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n   * the horizontal line \\(H:\\;y=1\\);\n   * for each \\(j\\in\\{k+1,k+2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the vertical line \\(V_j:\\;x=j\\);\n   * for each \\(i\\in I\\) the sunny line  \n\n     \\[\n     S_i:\\;y = x-i+1\\qquad(\\text{slope }1).\n     \\]\n\n   The family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{S_i\\mid i\\in I\\}\\) and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines (the horizontal line and the remaining vertical lines).\n\n   **Coverage proof.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(b=1\\)*, the point lies on \\(H\\).\n\n   *If \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(a\\ge k+1\\)*, the vertical line \\(V_a\\) is present, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on it.\n\n   *If \\(b\\ge2\\) and \\(1\\le a\\le k\\)*, we use the chain of sunny lines.  \n   For any \\(t\\ge0\\) with \\(a+t\\le k+1\\) the line \\(S_{a+t-1}\\) (when \\(t\\ge1\\)) passes through the point \\((a+t,b-t)\\) because  \n\n   \\[\n   b-t = (a+t)-(a+t-1)+1 = a-t+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Choose \\(t=b-1\\) (so that the second coordinate becomes \\(1\\)).  \n   Since \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and \\(a\\le k\\le n-2\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   a+(b-1)\\le a+(n-a)=n\\le k+1\\quad\\text{(because }k\\le n-2\\text{)} .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the line \\(