tal, and each sunny line contributes at most one point to each diagonal).\n\n   Consequently the total number of points that can be covered by any configuration with parameters \\((k,m)\\) satisfies\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{\\sum_{c\\in S}(c-1)}_{\\text{points on the chosen diagonals}}\n   \\;+\\;\n   \\underbrace{k\\cdot n}_{\\text{points possibly added by the other lines}}\n   \\;\\ge\\;\n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Optimising the left‑hand side of (2).**  \n\n   For a fixed \\(k\\) the left‑hand side is maximised when the set \\(S\\) consists of the **largest** possible diagonals, because larger diagonals contain more points.  \n   Since \\(|S|=m\\le n-k\\) (by (1)), the optimal choice is\n   \\[\n   S=\\{k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\},\n   \\]\n   i.e. the \\(n-k\\) largest values of \\(c\\).  \n   For this choice\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c\\in S}(c-1)=\\sum_{c=k+2}^{n+1}(c-1)\n   =\\sum_{j=k+1}^{n}j\n   =\\frac{(n+k+1)(n-k)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting into (2) gives the necessary inequality\n   \\[\n   k n+\\frac{(n+k+1)(n-k)}2\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Solving inequality (3).**  \n\n   Rearranging (3):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k(2n-k-1)}2\\;\\ge\\;0 .\n   \\]\n   Because \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) lines), the factor \\(2n-k-1\\) is positive, and the inequality holds with equality **only when \\(k=0\\)**.  \n   For any \\(k\\ge 1\\) the left‑hand side of (3) is **strictly larger** than \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\).\n\n   The strict inequality means that, even after using the optimal set of diagonal lines, the remaining \\(k\\) sunny (or horizontal/vertical) lines would be forced to supply more points than they are capable of (each can give at most one point per diagonal). Hence no configuration with \\(k\\ge 1\\) can cover exactly the set \\(T_n\\).\n\n8. **Existence for \\(k=0\\).**  \n\n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonal lines\n   \\[\n   \\ell_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad (c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n   \\]\n   They are pairwise distinct, each is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non‑sunny), and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\) in that range, so \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_c\\).  \n   Thus a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists.\n\nCombining the impossibility for any \\(k\\ge 1\\) with the explicit construction for \\(k=0\\) yields the complete classification.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The only admissible value is }k=0.}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
