nts.  \n   Since \\(|S|=t\\), the optimal choice is  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{\\,n+2-t,\\;n+3-t,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For this choice  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c\\in S}(c-1)=\\sum_{j=n+1-t}^{n}j\n   =\\frac{t\\bigl(2n+1-t\\bigr)}{2}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Deriving a necessary inequality for \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Substituting (4) into (3) and using \\(t\\le n-k\\) (from (2)) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   k n+\\frac{t\\bigl(2n+1-t\\bigr)}{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The left‑hand side of (5) is an increasing function of \\(t\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le n\\) (its derivative with respect to \\(t\\) is \\(\\frac{2n+1-2t}{2}\\ge 0\\) when \\(t\\le n\\)).  \n   Therefore the most favourable situation for a given \\(k\\) is to take the largest admissible \\(t\\), namely \\(t=n-k\\).  \n   Putting \\(t=n-k\\) into (5) yields\n\n   \\[\n   k n+\\frac{(n-k)\\bigl(2n+1-(n-k)\\bigr)}{2}\n   \\;\\ge\\;\n   \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying:\n\n   \\[\n   k n+\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   2kn+(n-k)(n+k+1)\\ge n^{2}-n\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   n^{2}+n+k^{2}+k\\ge 2n\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\\;\n   k^{2}+k\\le 2n. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (6) is **necessary** for the existence of a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n8. **Sufficiency: constructing a configuration whenever (6) holds.**  \n\n   Assume a non‑negative integer \\(k\\) satisfies \\(k^{2}+k\\le 2n\\).  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   t=n-k\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad S=\\{\\,k+2,k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Diagonal lines.*  \n   For each \\(c\\in S\\) take the diagonal line \\(\\ell_{c}:x+y=c\\).  \n   These are \\(t\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and together they cover all points of the diagonals \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(c\\in S\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   The remaining diagonals are \\(D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\).  \n   Diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points.  \n   Because of (6) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c=2}^{k+1}(c-1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the total number of points that still have to be covered does not exceed \\(n\\).  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each passing through a different uncovered point and otherwise in a generic direction (for instance, for the \\(i\\)-th uncovered point \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\) take the line of slope \\(\\sqrt{2}+i\\) through that point).  \n   Because the slopes are all distinct and none equals \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), every such line is sunny and no two of them coincide.  \n   Moreover, each sunny line meets each diagonal \\(D_{c}\\) in at most one point, so the already covered points are not disturbed.\n\n   Altogether we have used \\(t+(k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.  \n\n   Thus condition (6) is also **sufficient**.\n\n9. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   The admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the non‑negative integers satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   k^{2}+k\\le 2n .\n   \\]\n\n   Equivalently,\n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{1+8n}}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each such \\(k\\) an explicit construction has been given; for any larger \\(k\\) the necessary inequality (6) fails, so no configuration can exist.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\nk\\text{ is admissible }\\Longleftrightarrow k^{2}+k\\le 2n\n}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
