n contain at most one point from each diagonal.\n\n   *Non‑sunny lines* are of three types:\n   * horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)) and vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)) lines – they behave exactly like sunny lines with respect to the diagonals, i.e. at most one point per diagonal;\n   * lines parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) – they are precisely the diagonals \\(x+y=c\\) themselves and contain **all** points of the corresponding diagonal \\(D_{c}\\).\n\n4. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   * \\(k\\) = number of sunny lines (the quantity we are asked to determine);\n   * \\(t\\) = number of diagonal (non‑sunny) lines of the form \\(x+y=c\\) that are actually used.\n\n   Because the total number of distinct lines is \\(n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   k+t\\le n .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Covering the diagonals.**  \n\n   Suppose the chosen diagonal lines are \\(\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in S\\}\\) where \\(S\\subset\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and \\(|S|=t\\).  \n   Those lines cover all points of the diagonals \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(c\\in S\\).  \n   The remaining diagonals \\(\\{D_{c}\\mid c\\notin S\\}\\) must be covered by the other \\(k\\) lines (sunny, horizontal or vertical).  \n\n   Each of those \\(k\\) lines can meet **each uncovered diagonal** in at most one point, hence the total number of points that the sunny lines can supply to the uncovered diagonals is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,(n-t). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Counting points already covered.**  \n\n   For a fixed set \\(S\\) the number of points already covered by the diagonal lines equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c\\in S}(c-1). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   To obtain the most favourable situation for a given pair \\((k,t)\\) we maximise (4) by taking the largest possible values of \\(c\\); i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{\\,n+2-t,\\;n+3-t,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   With this choice  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c\\in S}(c-1)=\\frac{t\\bigl(2n+1-t\\bigr)}{2}. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Necessary inequality.**  \n\n   All points of \\(T_{n}\\) must be covered, so the points already covered (5) together with the maximal possible contribution of the sunny lines (3) must be at least \\(|T_{n}|\\) from (1):\n\n   \\[\n   k\\,(n-t)+\\frac{t\\bigl(2n+1-t\\bigr)}{2}\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The left‑hand side of (6) is an increasing function of \\(t\\) for \\(0\\le t\\le n\\) (its derivative with respect to \\(t\\) is \\(\\frac{2n+1-2t}{2}\\ge0\\) when \\(t\\le n\\)).  \n   Hence, for a given \\(k\\) the most permissive choice is the largest admissible \\(t\\), namely \\(t=n-k\\) (the equality case of (2)).  \n\n   Substituting \\(t=n-k\\) into (6) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k^{2}+\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   A straightforward simplification yields the *trivial* inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus the only necessary condition coming from the counting argument is \\(k\\le n\\); of course \\(k\\) must be a non‑negative integer.\n\n8. **Sufficiency: explicit construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).**  \n\n   Fix any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(t=n-k\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{\\,k+2,\\;k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\},\n   \\qquad |S|=t .\n   \\]\n\n   *Diagonal (non‑sunny) lines.*  \n   For each \\(c\\in S\\) take the line \\(\\ell_{c}:x+y=c\\).  \n   These are \\(t\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and they cover all points of the diagonals \\(D_{c}\\) with \\(c\\in S\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines.*  \n   The uncovered diagonals are \\(D_{2},D_{3},\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\).  \n   Their total number of points equals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{c=2}^{k+1}(c-1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\le n,\n   \\]\n\n   the inequality being a direct consequence of \\(k\\le n\\).  \n   Choose \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each passing through a different uncovered point, and give each line a slope that is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\) (for instance, for the \\(i\\)-th chosen point \\((a_{i},b_{i})\\) use the line of slope \\(\\sqrt{2}+i\\) through that point).  \n   Because the slopes are all distinct and avoid the three forbidden directions, all these lines are sunny and no two coincide.  \n   Each sunny line meets every diagonal in at most one point, so the points already covered by the diagonal lines are not lost.\n\n   Altogether we have used exactly \\(t+k=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_{n}\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n9. **Conclusion.**  \n\n   The necessary condition derived from the counting argument is \\(k\\le n\\); the construction above shows that this condition is also sufficient. Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   For each such \\(k\\) an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements has been exhibited.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
